INSERT INTO _s496_m1_c VALUES
(4990,1,1,$n$The beginning of the gospel of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ), the Son of Elohim (God);$n$,'romans',1,1,$n$thread:gospel-promised-afore | Mark opens with *the beginning of the gospel of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ), the Son of Elohim (God)* — naming the gospel Paul says was *promised afore by his prophets in the holy scriptures, concerning his Son.* The gospel does not begin at Mark 1:1; Mark 1:1 is the *beginning* of the documented account of a gospel the prophets had been carrying for centuries.$n$,$n$Romans 1:1 — *Paul, a servant of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ), called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of Elohim (God),* *The beginning of the gospel of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ), the Son of Elohim (God);* (Mark 1:1) Mark calls what he is writing *the beginning of the gospel*, and Paul opens his letter by saying he was set apart for that same gospel. Paul goes on to say where it came from: *Which he had promised afore by his prophets in the holy scriptures* (Romans 1:2). So Mark is not starting something new; he is writing down the opening of what the prophets had already promised.$n$),
(4993,1,3,$n$The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare you the way of Yahuah (Lord), make his paths straight.$n$,'malachi',4,5,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *Behold, I will send you Eliyahu (Elijah) the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (the LORD).* The Eliyahu-pattern prophecy lands on Yochanan (John); the King names him explicitly as such at Mark 9:13.$n$,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* *The voice of one crying in the wilderness, Prepare you the way of Yahuah (Lord), make his paths straight.* (Mark 1:3) Mark hears Isaiah's *voice of one crying in the wilderness* and sets it at the Jordan, and Malachi says whose voice it is. Yahuah (the LORD) will send *Elijah the prophet* ahead of *the great and dreadful day*, and John comes out of the wilderness as that forerunner, as Mark himself goes on to say.$n$),
(5002,1,22,$n$And they were astonished at his doctrine: for he taught them as one that had authority, and not as the scribes.$n$,'mark',7,13,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *He taught them as one that had authority, and not as the scribes.* The scribes taught by appeal to the chain of tradition Mark 7:13 names as the Pharisaic voiding of Torah — *making the word of Yahuah (God) of none effect through your tradition.* The King teaches with the authority of the Word himself.$n$,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* *And they were astonished at his doctrine: for he taught them as one that had authority, and not as the scribes.* (Mark 1:22) The synagogue heard the difference at once: he taught *as one that had authority, and not as the scribes*. Behind the scribes stood a tradition handed down from the elders, and here Yahusha (Jesus) says what that tradition had done, that it made the word of Elohim (God) of none effect. He spoke the commandment itself, and they had been speaking around it.$n$),
(5003,1,38,$n$And he said unto them, Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.$n$,'matthew',15,24,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | *Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.* The *therefore came I forth* statement names the mission-scope as the gathering-of-the-lost-sheep Matthew 15:24 articulates directly — *I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).*$n$,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *And he said unto them, Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.* (Mark 1:38) Mark gives what he was sent for in four words, *therefore came I forth*, and Matthew says in plain words who he was sent to. When Yahusha (Jesus) leaves the crowd that was looking for him and goes on to the next towns, he is going to the lost sheep of Yashar'el's (Israel's) scattered house, wherever they are found.$n$),
(5004,1,38,$n$And he said unto them, Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.$n$,'ezekiel',34,11,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | *I myself, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* The next-towns expansion is going where the sheep are; Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 34's shepherd-prophecy is the substance the King walks.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* *And he said unto them, Let us go into the next towns, that I may preach there also: for therefore came I forth.* (Mark 1:38) Ezekiel hears Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) say *I, even I,* will search for his sheep, when the shepherds had fed themselves and left them. Here Yahusha (Jesus) will not stay where he is wanted and says *therefore came I forth*; he goes out to do the seeking Ezekiel heard promised.$n$),
(5005,1,40,$n$And there came a leper to him, beseeching him, and kneeling down to him, and saying unto him, If you will, you can make me clean.$n$,'leviticus',14,2,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | The leper's cleansing leads directly to the Torah-affirming closure of Mark 1:44 — *shew thyself to the priest, and offer for thy cleansing those things which Mosheh (Moses) commanded.* The King's healing does not abolish the Vayikra (Leviticus) 14 cleansing-protocol; it sends the healed man into it.$n$,$n$Leviticus 14:2 — *This shall be the law of the leper in the day of his cleansing: He shall be brought unto the priest:* *And there came a leper to him, beseeching him, and kneeling down to him, and saying unto him, If you will, you can make me clean.* (Mark 1:40) The leper asks to be made clean, and Yahusha (Jesus) makes him clean and then sends him on: *shew thyself to the priest, and offer for thy cleansing those things which Moses commanded* (Mark 1:44). Leviticus is where Moses wrote that law, with the priest at its door. He does not set it aside; he sends the man into it.$n$),
(5010,2,14,$n$And as he passed by, he saw Levi the son of Alphæus sitting at the receipt of custom, and said unto him, Follow me. And he arose and followed him.$n$,'ezekiel',34,16,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | *Follow me. And he arose and followed him.* Levi the publican at the receipt of custom is the kind of *sinner* the Pharisaic system had excluded — and the King calls him into the gathering-architecture. Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 34:16's *I will seek that which was lost, and bring again that which was driven away* is the Father's voice in flesh going where the lost sheep sit.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:16 — *I will seek that which was lost, and bring again that which was driven away, and will bind up that which was broken, and will strengthen that which was sick: but I will destroy the fat and the strong; I will feed them with judgment.* *And as he passed by, he saw Levi the son of Alphæus sitting at the receipt of custom, and said unto him, Follow me. And he arose and followed him.* (Mark 2:14) Levi sits at the tax booth, a man the town had written off, and Yahusha (Jesus) stops at his seat and says *Follow me*. Ezekiel heard the shepherd promise to *seek that which was lost*, and here the seeking reaches a man at his work, and he gets up from his booth and goes after him.$n$),
(5011,2,17,$n$When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he says unto them, They that are whole have no need of the physician, but they that are sick: I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.$n$,'ezekiel',34,4,$n$thread:lost-sheep-mission-scope | *I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* The mission scope is named in the same shape as Matthew 9:13 — the physician goes to the sick, the shepherd goes to the lost. Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 34:4's indictment of the shepherds who *strengthened not the diseased, neither healed that which was sick* names the failure the King is reversing in real time.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:4 — *The diseased have ye not strengthened, neither have ye healed that which was sick, neither have ye bound up that which was broken, neither have ye brought again that which was driven away, neither have ye sought that which was lost; but with force and with cruelty have ye ruled them.* *When Yahusha (Jesus) heard it, he says unto them, They that are whole have no need of the physician, but they that are sick: I came not to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance.* (Mark 2:17) When the complaint about his company reaches him, he answers that the whole have no need of a physician, and that he came to call sinners to repentance. Ezekiel had charged the shepherds of Yashar'el (Israel) with leaving the sick unhealed and the lost unsought, and ruling the flock with force. Yahusha (Jesus) goes to the very sheep that charge describes.$n$),
(5012,2,19,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them? as long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.$n$,'hosea',2,19,$n$thread:bridegroom-messianic-identification | *Can the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them?* The bridegroom self-identification ties Mark 2:19 into Hoshea (Hosea) 2:19-20's *I will betroth thee unto me for ever.*$n$,$n$Hosea 2:19 — *And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, Can the children of the bridechamber fast, while the bridegroom is with them? as long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast.* (Mark 2:19) He calls himself the bridegroom, and while the bridegroom is with them the guests cannot fast. Hosea had already told whose wedding this is: Yahuah (the LORD) says to the house he put away, *I will betroth thee unto me for ever*, in righteousness and in mercies. The gladness that keeps the guests from fasting is that promise being kept to the scattered house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
(5016,2,23,$n$And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.$n$,'leviticus',19,9,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | *Behold, why do they on the sabbath day that which is not lawful?* The accusation is built on the Pharisaic fence — the tradition that named gleaning-as-you-walked as harvest-labor. The Torah of Vayikra (Leviticus) 19:9-10 explicitly preserved field-gleanings for the hungry; nothing in Torah forbade what the disciples did.$n$,$n$Leviticus 19:9 — *And when ye reap the harvest of your land, thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field, neither shalt thou gather the gleanings of thy harvest.* *And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.* (Mark 2:23) The disciples are hungry and pluck the ears as they go, and the charge is that this is not lawful on the sabbath. Leviticus shows how the Torah itself treats a field: the corners and the gleanings are left standing for the poor, so hunger in a field is something the commandments make room for, not something they forbid.$n$),
(5017,2,23,$n$And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.$n$,'deuteronomy',23,25,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | *When thou comest into the standing corn of thy neighbour, then thou mayest pluck the ears with thine hand.* Devarim (Deuteronomy) 23:25 explicitly permits what the disciples did; the accusation is fence-not-Torah.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 23:25 — *When thou comest into the standing corn of thy neighbour, then thou mayest pluck the ears with thine hand; but thou shalt not move a sickle unto thy neighbour’s standing corn.* *And it came to pass, that he went through the corn fields on the sabbath day; and his disciples began, as they went, to pluck the ears of corn.* (Mark 2:23) This is the verse the disciples were living by. A man walking through his neighbour's standing corn may take the ears in his hand, only he may not put a sickle to it, and that is just what they did, ears by hand as they went. The charge against them rests on a rule laid over the day, not on the commandment about the day.$n$),
(5018,2,25,$n$And he said unto them, Have you never read what David did, when he had need, and was an hungred, he, and they that were with him?$n$,'1-samuel',21,1,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | The King answers with the David-at-Nob precedent — David and his men eating the shewbread when in need, and Yahuah's house preserving the man over the ritual. The pattern is the same: mercy and life take precedence over the fence the system built around the rite.$n$,$n$1 Samuel 21:1 — *Then came David to Nob to Ahimelech the priest: and Ahimelech was afraid at the meeting of David, and said unto him, Why art thou alone, and no man with thee?* *And he said unto them, Have you never read what David did, when he had need, and was an hungred, he, and they that were with him?* (Mark 2:25) He sends them back to a page they all knew. David comes to Nob hungry with his men, and the priest tells him *There is no common bread under mine hand, but there is hallowed bread* (1 Samuel 21:4). The hungry men are fed from the bread that was set apart, by the priest who had it to give. That is the story Yahusha (Jesus) lays beside the disciples in the grain.$n$),
(5022,3,1,$n$And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.$n$,'exodus',20,8,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | The withered-hand healing on the Sabbath asks *Is it lawful to do good on the sabbath days, or to do evil? to save life, or to kill?* and exposes the Pharisaic fence that called healing-on-the-Sabbath unlawful while leaving the man broken. The Torah's Sabbath-command protects life and rest; the fence inverted the command into the prohibition of mercy.$n$,$n$Exodus 20:8 — *Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy.* *And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.* (Mark 3:1) The man is in the synagogue on the day the commandment says to keep holy, and Yahusha (Jesus) does not put that commandment aside to help him. He puts the question to the room: *Is it lawful to do good on the sabbath days, or to do evil?* (Mark 3:4). The day stands; what he refuses is the added rule that would have left the man as he was.$n$),
(5023,3,1,$n$And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.$n$,'deuteronomy',5,12,$n$thread:shabbat-controversy-heart-shabbat-vs-pharisaic-fence | *Keep the sabbath day to sanctify it.* The Devarim (Deuteronomy) re-naming of the Sabbath-commandment carries the same heart-of-Torah substance the King recovers against the Pharisaic fence.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 5:12 — *Keep the sabbath day to sanctify it, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee.* *And he entered again into the synagogue; and there was a man there which had a withered hand.* (Mark 3:1) Yahusha (Jesus) goes into the synagogue on the sabbath as a man keeping the day, and Moses gave that commandment a second time in Deuteronomy, *as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee*. The healing happens inside the keeping, in the assembly of the day, and not on his way out of it.$n$),
(5024,3,6,$n$And the Pharisees went forth, and straightway took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him.$n$,'john',11,47,$n$thread:pharisaic-sadducean-system-as-judaizer-predecessor | *The Pharisees went forth, and straightway took counsel with the Herodians against him.* The Pharisee-Herodian alliance foreshadows the same flesh-credential-system-plus-political-power architecture the Galatian agitators and modern institutional Christianity both rest on.$n$,$n$John 11:47 — *Then gathered the chief priests and the Pharisees a council, and said, What do we? for this man doeth many miracles.* *And the Pharisees went forth, and straightway took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him.* (Mark 3:6) Here the Pharisees go out and plot with the Herodians to destroy the man who had just made a hand whole. John shows the same thing from the other end: after Lazarus is raised, the chief priests and Pharisees gather a council and ask *What do we?* because *this man doeth many miracles*. In both rooms the good that was done is the problem they meet to solve.$n$),
(5027,3,14,$n$And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,$n$,'ezekiel',37,15,$n$thread:twelve-apostles-twelve-tribes-restoration-architecture | *He ordained twelve, that they should be with him.* The number twelve is not coincidence; the twelve apostles correspond to the twelve tribes of the prophesied restoration (Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 37:15-22's two-sticks-becoming-one).$n$,$n$Ezekiel 37:15 — *The word of Yahuah (LORD) came again unto me, saying,* *And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,* (Mark 3:14) Yahusha (Jesus) names twelve on the mountain, and twelve is the number of the tribes. Ezekiel is told to take two sticks, one for Yahudah (Judah) and one for the house of Yashar'el (Israel), and the word is *join them one to another into one stick; and they shall become one in thine hand* (Ezekiel 37:17). Twelve men are sent out to a house that is to be made one again.$n$),
(5028,3,14,$n$And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,$n$,'revelation',21,12,$n$thread:twelve-apostles-twelve-tribes-restoration-architecture | *The wall of the city had twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.* The Revelation 21 New-Yerushalayim architecture names twelve foundations for the twelve apostles, twelve gates for the twelve tribes — the same architecture Mark 3:14 inaugurates.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel):* *And he ordained twelve, that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach,* (Mark 3:14) Revelation sets twelve gates in the wall of the holy city, and on them the names of *the twelve tribes*, and under the wall *twelve foundations, and in them the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb* (Revelation 21:14). The tribes are written on the gates and the men who were sent are written in the foundations, so the number Yahusha (Jesus) ordained on the mountain is the number the city is built on.$n$),
(5029,3,22,$n$And the scribes which came down from Jerusalem said, He has Beelzebub, and by the prince of the devils casts he out devils.$n$,'mark',7,13,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *The scribes which came down from Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) said, He hath Beelzebub.* The same Pharisaic system that voided Torah by tradition (Mark 7:13) now ascribes the Spirit-work to the unclean spirit.$n$,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* *And the scribes which came down from Jerusalem said, He has Beelzebub, and by the prince of the devils casts he out devils.* (Mark 3:22) Here scribes from Jerusalem watch a man set free from unclean spirits and say his power is Beelzebub's. Yahusha (Jesus) says elsewhere what happens when a teaching puts its own handed-down tradition above the commandment: it makes the word of Elohim (God) of none effect. When the teaching of men matters more than the word, even a man made whole is read as a danger.$n$),
(5030,3,28,$n$Verily I say unto you, All sins shall be forgiven unto the sons of men, and blasphemies wherewith soever they shall blaspheme:$n$,'1-john',5,16,$n$thread:blasphemy-against-the-ruach-warning | *He that shall blaspheme against the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) hath never forgiveness.* The warning fits 1 Yochanan (John) 5:16-17's *there is a sin unto death* — the hardened disposition that calls the Spirit-work unclean. The mercy is wide; the deliberate calling-of-the-Spirit-unclean is the door closing from the inside.$n$,$n$1 John 5:16 — *If any man see his brother sin a sin which is not unto death, he shall ask, and he shall give him life for them that sin not unto death. There is a sin unto death: I do not say that he shall pray for it.* *Verily I say unto you, All sins shall be forgiven unto the sons of men, and blasphemies wherewith soever they shall blaspheme:* (Mark 3:28) Mark opens wide: every sin and every blasphemy shall be forgiven, and then he sets one thing outside that forgiveness. John draws a line of his own among sins. When a man sees his brother sin a sin not unto death, he asks and life is given, but there is *a sin unto death*, and John says he does not say to pray for it.$n$),
(5031,3,33,$n$And he answered them, saying, Who is my mother, or my brethren?$n$,'hosea',1,10,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *Whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother.* The kingdom-family is defined by doing-the-Father's-will, not by paternal blood claim alone. The kinship-redefinition is the post-Lo-Ammi naming Hoshea (Hosea) 1:10 promised — *ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God).*$n$,$n$Hosea 1:10 — *Yet the number of the children of Yashar'el (Israel) shall be as the sand of the sea, which cannot be measured nor numbered; and it shall come to pass, that in the place where it was said unto them, Ye are not my people, there it shall be said unto them, Ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God).* *And he answered them, saying, Who is my mother, or my brethren?* (Mark 3:33) Asked who his mother and brethren are, he looks round at those sitting with him and says that whoever does *the will of Elohim (God)* (Mark 3:35) is that family. Hosea gives the other side: those once told *Ye are not my people* are called *sons of the living Elohim (God)*, and the Father does the calling. The doing is how such sons are known when they stand before him; it is the Father who has named them.$n$),
(5032,3,35,$n$For whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother.$n$,'matthew',7,21,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *Not every one that saith unto me, Lord, Lord, shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father.* The Matthean parallel of the doing-the-will-as-kingdom-membership architecture; same heart-Torah substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 7:21 — *Not every one that saith unto me, Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord), shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.* *For whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother.* (Mark 3:35) Mark's family is made of those who do *the will of Elohim (God)*, and Matthew has him say it in his own words at the end of the sermon: the door is for *he that doeth the will of my Father*. There the saying carries a warning against calling him Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord) without doing. The doing is what shows who belongs, whether he is naming his household or naming who enters.$n$),
(5034,4,3,$n$Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow:$n$,'john',10,26,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | The sower's seed falling on four ground-types is the cause-and-effect-of-hearing architecture — the seed is the same in every case (the Word); the difference is the ground. Yochanan (John) 10:26-27's *they believe not, because they are not of my sheep... my sheep hear my voice* names the same diagnostic the parable enacts.$n$,$n$John 10:26 — *But ye believe not, because ye are not of my sheep, as I said unto you.* *Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow:* (Mark 4:3) A sower goes out and the same seed falls on every kind of ground, and the first thing he says is *Hearken*. John gives the reason some hear and some do not: *ye are not of my sheep*, and the next verse says *My sheep hear my voice* (John 10:27). The hearing shows what the hearer already was; it does not make him that.$n$),
(5036,4,14,$n$The sower sows the word.$n$,'matthew',13,18,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | The four soils are not four salvation-stages but four reveal-patterns. The good ground bringing forth fruit names the wheat that survives both the harvest-separation (Matthew 13:30) and the post-harvest sifting.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:18 — *Hear ye therefore the parable of the sower.* *The sower sows the word.* (Mark 4:14) Mark gives the key in five words, *The sower soweth the word.* Matthew sets the same explanation after he has told the disciples their eyes and ears are blessed, and then opens it with *Hear ye therefore*. So the explanation is given to those who hear, and in Matthew what is sown is *the word of the kingdom* (Matthew 13:19), met by four kinds of ground.$n$),
(5037,4,14,$n$The sower sows the word.$n$,'ezekiel',20,37,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | *I will cause you to pass under the rod, and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant: and I will purge out from among you the rebels.* The post-harvest sifting Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) names is the architecture the soils-parable enacts.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 20:37 — *And I will cause you to pass under the rod, and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant:* *The sower sows the word.* (Mark 4:14) The sower sows the word, and the grounds show what each hearer does with it. Ezekiel hears Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) tell the house he has gathered that they will *pass under the rod* before he brings them into the bond of the covenant, and that he will *purge out from among you the rebels* (Ezekiel 20:38). Laid beside the soils, the rod is the testing of those already gathered, to see what the word they received has borne.$n$),
(5038,4,17,$n$And have no root in themselves, and so endure but for a time: afterward, when affliction or persecution arises for the word’s sake, immediately they are offended.$n$,'hebrews',6,4,$n$thread:grace-and-the-opportunity-that-can-be-lost | *They have no root in themselves, and so endure but for a time.* The stony-ground hearer received the Word with gladness and did not endure — naming the same warning Hebrews 6:4-6 carries. The gift was real; the persistence was not. The opportunity that can be lost is the post-Reformation truncation's missing clause.$n$,$n$Hebrews 6:4 — *For it is impossible for those who were once enlightened, and have tasted of the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit),* *And have no root in themselves, and so endure but for a time: afterward, when affliction or persecution arises for the word’s sake, immediately they are offended.* (Mark 4:17) The rocky ground hears the word and receives it gladly, but it has no root, and when trouble comes for the word's sake it gives way. Hebrews describes people who stood that near: enlightened, tasting the heavenly gift, made partakers of the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit). Then the writer says that if they fall away it is impossible *to renew them again unto repentance* (Hebrews 6:6). Both texts speak of a real loss.$n$),
(5039,4,19,$n$And the cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts of other things entering in, choke the word, and it becomes unfruitful.$n$,'1-timothy',6,9,$n$thread:grace-and-the-opportunity-that-can-be-lost | *The cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches... choke the word.* The thorny-ground hearer's loss matches 1 Timothy 6:9-10's *they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare.*$n$,$n$1 Timothy 6:9 — *But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition.* *And the cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts of other things entering in, choke the word, and it becomes unfruitful.* (Mark 4:19) The thorns in the parable are worldly cares, the pull of riches and the lusts for other things, and what they choke is the word, so that it becomes unfruitful. Paul tells Timothy what the same ground does to a man: those who will be rich *fall into temptation and a snare* and are drowned in *destruction and perdition*. The word was sown in him and the thorns took it, which is why the warning is given.$n$),
(5040,4,22,$n$For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad.$n$,'ecclesiastes',12,14,$n$thread:i-know-thy-works-and-names-blotted-out | *There is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested.* The manifestation-at-the-end pattern names what Ecclesiastes 12:14 names — *for Elohim (God) shall bring every work into judgment, with every secret thing.*$n$,$n$Ecclesiastes 12:14 — *For Elohim (God) shall bring every work into judgment, with every secret thing, whether it be good, or whether it be evil.* *For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad.* (Mark 4:22) Mark sets this saying right after the lamp that is not put under a bushel: what is covered is meant to come out. The Preacher closes his book the same way, saying that Elohim (God) will bring every work into judgment, with *every secret thing*, whether it be good or evil.$n$),
(5042,4,25,$n$For he that has, to him shall be given: and he that has not, from him shall be taken even that which he has.$n$,'revelation',13,8,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *He that hath, to him shall be given.* Names get blotted out from the wheat; the tares never had names to begin with (Revelation 13:8 / 17:8 — *whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world*).$n$,$n$Revelation 13:8 — *And all that dwell upon the earth shall worship him, whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world.* *For he that has, to him shall be given: and he that has not, from him shall be taken even that which he has.* (Mark 4:25) Mark's saying about having and not having is hard to hear alone. Beside it, Revelation says that those who worship the beast are the ones *whose names are not written in the book of life*, and that the book is the Lamb's and was written from the foundation of the world. The book was settled first, and what shows in a man afterward is what the book already held.$n$),
(5045,4,35,$n$And the same day, when the even was come, he says unto them, Let us pass over unto the other side.$n$,'psalms',89,9,$n$thread:walking-on-the-sea-formed-cloud-rider | The wind-and-sea-stilling of Mark 4:35-41 is the Yahuah-prerogative Tehillim (Psalm) 89:9 names — *Thou rulest the raging of the sea: when the waves thereof arise, thou stillest them.*$n$,$n$Psalm 89:9 — *Thou rulest the raging of the sea: when the waves thereof arise, thou stillest them.* *And the same day, when the even was come, he says unto them, Let us pass over unto the other side.* (Mark 4:35) He tells them to cross over, and when the storm rises he rebukes the wind and says to the sea *Peace, be still* (Mark 4:39). The psalm tells whose work that is: of Yahuah (the LORD) of hosts it says that when the waves arise, *thou stillest them*. The disciples ask what manner of man this is, and he has just done what the psalm gives to Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
(5049,5,7,$n$And cried with a loud voice, and said, What have I to do with you, Yahusha (Jesus), you Son of the El Elyon (most high God)? I adjure you by Elohim (God), that you torment me not.$n$,'james',2,19,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | *Son of the El Elyon (most high God)* — the unclean spirit names what the religious leaders refused to name. The demons confess; the elders do not. *The devils also believe, and tremble* (James 2:19).$n$,$n$James 2:19 — *Thou believest that there is one Elohim (God); thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble.* *And cried with a loud voice, and said, What have I to do with you, Yahusha (Jesus), you Son of the El Elyon (most high God)? I adjure you by Elohim (God), that you torment me not.* (Mark 5:7) The spirit knows exactly who Yahusha (Jesus) is and says it out loud, and James says the same of the devils, that they *believe, and tremble*. Naming him rightly costs the spirit nothing, and it is not the same as belonging to him or doing what he says.$n$),
(5050,5,7,$n$And cried with a loud voice, and said, What have I to do with you, Yahusha (Jesus), you Son of the El Elyon (most high God)? I adjure you by Elohim (God), that you torment me not.$n$,'genesis',14,18,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | *Son of the El Elyon* — the same Most-High naming the Melek-Tsadiq (Melchizedek) encounter at Bereshit (Genesis) 14:18-20 attached to. The Formed-in-flesh stands in front of the Watcher-rebellion administration that has held the man bound.$n$,$n$Genesis 14:18 — *And Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) king of Salem brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the El Elyon (most high God).* *And cried with a loud voice, and said, What have I to do with you, Yahusha (Jesus), you Son of the El Elyon (most high God)? I adjure you by Elohim (God), that you torment me not.* (Mark 5:7) The spirit calls him Son of the Most High, and the title goes back to Abram's day. Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) met him as *the priest of the El Elyon (most high God)*, so the one the legion begs by is the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the man in the tombs is standing in front of his Son.$n$),
(5051,5,23,$n$And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lies at the point of death: I pray you, come and lay your hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live.$n$,'matthew',9,18,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | *My little daughter lieth at the point of death... come and lay thy hands on her, that she may be healed.* Jairus the ruler of the synagogue falls at the King's feet — the synagogue official kneels in front of the One the system would shortly reject. The recognition crosses the line from marginalized to magistrate; the desperation is the same.$n$,$n$Matthew 9:18 — *While he spake these things unto them, behold, there came a certain ruler, and worshipped him, saying, My daughter is even now dead: but come and lay thy hand upon her, and she shall live.* *And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lies at the point of death: I pray you, come and lay your hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live.* (Mark 5:23) Matthew has the ruler say his daughter *is even now dead*. Mark has her still living, *at the point of death*, and the father pleading greatly for Yahusha (Jesus) to come while there is time. Mark lets us stand in the hour before the news comes from the house.$n$),
(5052,5,23,$n$And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lies at the point of death: I pray you, come and lay your hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live.$n$,'luke',8,41,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | The Lukan parallel of the synagogue-ruler's desperation at Mark 5:23; the recognition-by-marginalized broadened to include desperation-from-magistrate.$n$,$n$Luke 8:41 — *And, behold, there came a man named Jairus, and he was a ruler of the synagogue: and he fell down at Yahusha's (Jesus’) feet, and besought him that he would come into his house:* *And besought him greatly, saying, My little daughter lies at the point of death: I pray you, come and lay your hands on her, that she may be healed; and she shall live.* (Mark 5:23) Mark gives the father's words about the girl, and Luke tells us where he wanted Yahusha (Jesus) to go, *into his house*. He was *a ruler of the synagogue*, a man of standing, and he fell down at the feet of the one the rulers would soon turn against.$n$),
(5053,5,25,$n$And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,$n$,'leviticus',15,25,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | *A certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years.* Vayikra (Leviticus) 15:25-30 names the woman's flow as continually unclean — barred from synagogue, barred from contact, barred from worship. The Torah-defined uncleanness does not flow into the King when she touches the hem; cleanness flows out of him into her.$n$,$n$Leviticus 15:25 — *And if a woman have an issue of her blood many days out of the time of her separation, or if it run beyond the time of her separation; all the days of the issue of her uncleanness shall be as the days of her separation: she shall be unclean.* *And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,* (Mark 5:25) Moses wrote the rule down, and nothing in Mark says it was wrong. For as long as the issue ran she was unclean, and for her that meant twelve years. What Mark shows is that when she touched Yahusha (Jesus) the cleanness went out of him into her, and the uncleanness did not pass to him.$n$),
(5054,5,25,$n$And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,$n$,'numbers',15,38,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | *Behind, and touched his garment.* The fringe she reaches for is the Bamidbar (Numbers) 15:38-39 tzitzit of the King's prayer-shawl — the Torah's appointed reminder-fringe.$n$,$n$Numbers 15:38 — *Speak unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and bid them that they make them fringes in the borders of their garments throughout their generations, and that they put upon the fringe of the borders a ribband of blue:* *And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,* (Mark 5:25) Mark does not say yet what she touched, but Numbers says what a garment of a man of Yashar'el (Israel) carried, *fringes in the borders of their garments* with a blue cord, there to make him remember the commandments. She comes up behind a man wearing what Moses commanded.$n$),
(5055,5,25,$n$And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,$n$,'jeremiah',23,6,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | The Torah's clean / unclean architecture is fulfilled by a cleansing the Torah pointed toward — *and his name shall be called Yahuah-Tsidkenu (THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS)* (Yirmeyahu/Jeremiah 23:6).$n$,$n$Jeremiah 23:6 — *In his days Yahudah (Judah) shall be saved, and Yashar'el (Israel) shall dwell safely: and this is his name whereby he shall be called, Yahuah Tsidkenu (THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS).* *And a certain woman, which had an issue of blood twelve years,* (Mark 5:25) Jeremiah promises a righteous Branch for David, and in his days *Yahudah (Judah) shall be saved, and Yashar'el (Israel) shall dwell safely*. A woman shut out for twelve years by the uncleanness Leviticus names is made whole by touching the one who carries the name Jeremiah gives that Branch, *Yahuah Tsidkenu (THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS)*.$n$),
(5056,5,34,$n$And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.$n$,'habakkuk',2,4,$n$thread:just-shall-live-by-his-faithfulness | *Daughter, thy faith hath made thee whole.* *Daughter* is the King naming her in the seed-of-promise. Her *emunah* (covenant trust) was the walking-out; the King names it explicitly. Trust in the Formed-in-flesh is the same trust Chavaqquq (Habakkuk) 2:4 named — *the just shall live by his faithfulness.*$n$,$n$Habakkuk 2:4 — *Behold, his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith.* *And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.* (Mark 5:34) He calls her *Daughter* in front of the crowd, and tells her it was her trust. Habakkuk says the just shall live by his faith, and what the woman did is that faith walked out. She heard of him, came up behind, and reached for the border of his garment.$n$),
(5057,5,34,$n$And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.$n$,'psalms',27,13,$n$thread:just-shall-live-by-his-faithfulness | *I had fainted, unless I had believed to see the goodness of Yahuah (the LORD) in the land of the living* — the Tehillim (Psalm) 27:13-14 trust-architecture the King is honoring in the woman.$n$,$n$Psalm 27:13 — *I had fainted, unless I had believed to see the goodness of Yahuah (LORD) in the land of the living.* *And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.* (Mark 5:34) The woman had tried everything for twelve years and was still failing, and the psalmist knows that place. He says *I had fainted* unless he believed he would see the goodness of Yahuah (the LORD) in *the land of the living*. She believed she would see it in her own body while she lived.$n$),
(5058,5,34,$n$And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.$n$,'isaiah',7,9,$n$thread:just-shall-live-by-his-faithfulness | *If ye will not believe, surely ye shall not be established* — Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 7:9's emunah-architecture in compressed form.$n$,$n$Isaiah 7:9 — *And the head of Ephraim is Samaria, and the head of Samaria is Remaliah’s son. If ye will not believe, surely ye shall not be established.* *And he said unto her, Daughter, your faith has made you whole; go in peace, and be whole of your plague.* (Mark 5:34) The verse that says *surely ye shall not be established* unless you believe is the one that names Ephraim, the house about to be scattered. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the woman her believing made her whole, and in a few verses he asks the same of a ruler.$n$),
(5059,5,36,$n$As soon as Yahusha (Jesus) heard the word that was spoken, he says unto the ruler of the synagogue, Be not afraid, only believe.$n$,'psalms',27,13,$n$thread:just-shall-live-by-his-faithfulness | *Be not afraid, only believe.* The same charge of emunah given to the synagogue ruler now hearing his daughter is dead; the King hands him exactly what the woman was just commended for.$n$,$n$Psalm 27:13 — *I had fainted, unless I had believed to see the goodness of Yahuah (LORD) in the land of the living.* *As soon as Yahusha (Jesus) heard the word that was spoken, he says unto the ruler of the synagogue, Be not afraid, only believe.* (Mark 5:36) Word has just come that the girl is dead, and Yahusha (Jesus) answers the father with the trust that kept the psalmist from fainting, believing *to see the goodness* of Yahuah (the LORD) in *the land of the living*. Jairus is asked to go on believing while the report says there is nothing left to see.$n$),
(5063,6,4,$n$But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.$n$,'1-kings',19,10,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | The Eliyahu (Elijah)-pattern carried at 1 Kings 19:10 — the prophet hunted by the king for naming the king's sin; the same pattern lands on Yochanan.$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:10 — *And he said, I have been very jealous for Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) of hosts: for the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have forsaken thy covenant, thrown down thine altars, and slain thy prophets with the sword; and I, even I only, am left; and they seek my life, to take it away.* *But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.* (Mark 6:4) Yahusha (Jesus) says a prophet has no honour *in his own country*, and Elijah's cry on Horeb shows how far that goes. He tells Yahuah (the LORD) that the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have *slain thy prophets with the sword* and are now after his own life. Nazareth's offence is the mild end of a long record.$n$),
(5064,6,4,$n$But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.$n$,'matthew',13,57,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house* — the Matthean parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 13:57 — *And they were offended in him. But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house.* *But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.* (Mark 6:4) Mark has one more circle than Matthew. Matthew gives *his own country, and in his own house*, and Mark adds *among his own kin*, so the refusal reaches past the town to the relatives. Both tell it right after the offence, with Matthew's *they were offended in him* leading straight into the saying.$n$),
(5065,6,4,$n$But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.$n$,'luke',4,24,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *No prophet is accepted in his own country* — the Lukan parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 4:24 — *And he said, Verily I say unto you, No prophet is accepted in his own country.* *But Yahusha (Jesus) said unto them, A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country, and among his own kin, and in his own house.* (Mark 6:4) Luke has *accepted* where Mark has honour, and he lets the saying open an argument. Straight after it come the widow at Sarepta and Naaman the Syrian, the two people Elijah and Elisha were sent to when many in Yashar'el (Israel) were passed over, so Luke makes the saying about whom a prophet is sent to.$n$),
(5067,6,7,$n$And he called unto him the twelve, and began to send them forth by two and two; and gave them power over unclean spirits;$n$,'matthew',10,1,$n$thread:twelve-apostles-twelve-tribes-restoration-architecture | The Matthean parallel sending-of-the-twelve.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:1 — *And when he had called unto him his twelve disciples, he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease.* *And he called unto him the twelve, and began to send them forth by two and two; and gave them power over unclean spirits;* (Mark 6:7) Matthew spells out the power the twelve carried, *to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease*, as well as over unclean spirits. Mark has the power over unclean spirits and one detail Matthew leaves out here, that he sent them *by two and two*, so that none of them went alone.$n$),
(5068,6,7,$n$And he called unto him the twelve, and began to send them forth by two and two; and gave them power over unclean spirits;$n$,'revelation',21,12,$n$thread:twelve-apostles-twelve-tribes-restoration-architecture | The twelve foundations of New-Yerushalayim bearing the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *And had a wall great and high, and had twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel):* *And he called unto him the twelve, and began to send them forth by two and two; and gave them power over unclean spirits;* (Mark 6:7) Twelve men are called and sent out, and the city in Revelation has *twelve gates* with the names of *the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel)* written on them. The gates carry the tribes, and the number Mark opens with is the number of the tribes.$n$),
(5069,6,11,$n$And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.$n$,'matthew',10,14,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | *Shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them.* The Matthean parallel; the prophetic-witness-act when the rejection lands.$n$,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.* (Mark 6:11) Mark gives the dust a purpose that Matthew's verse leaves out here, *for a testimony against them*, so the shaking is first of all a witness. He also puts the saying about Sodom and Gomorrah in the same breath as the departure, where Matthew sets it one verse later.$n$),
(5070,6,11,$n$And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.$n$,'acts',13,51,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | Paul and Barnabas shake the dust off in Acts 13:51 — the apostolic walk of the same charge.$n$,$n$Acts 13:51 — *But they shook off the dust of their feet against them, and came unto Iconium.* *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.* (Mark 6:11) Paul and Barnabas do what Mark describes, and Luke writes it as a plain act: they *shook off the dust of their feet against them* and went on to Iconium. The testimony is given and the road goes on, and the next verse has the disciples filled with joy and with the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit).$n$),
(5071,6,11,$n$And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.$n$,'john',10,27,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | *My sheep hear my voice.* Those who do not, the dust is shaken off — pearls before swine refused.$n$,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.* (Mark 6:11) Mark's *nor hear you* is the case where the word was not heard, and John gives the other side. *My sheep hear my voice*, so the hearing shows who they already were. The twelve go out broadly and leave the dust behind where nothing is heard, and nobody is chased.$n$),
(5072,6,11,$n$And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.$n$,'matthew',7,6,$n$thread:shake-the-dust-off-rejection-response | *Give not that which is holy unto the dogs, neither cast ye your pearls before swine* — the same charge in compressed form.$n$,$n$Matthew 7:6 — *Give not that which is holy unto the dogs, neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn again and rend you.* *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear you, when you depart thence, shake off the dust under your feet for a testimony against them. Verily I say unto you, It shall be more tolerable for Sodom and Gomorrah in the day of judgment, than for that city.* (Mark 6:11) Matthew has the warning that what is holy is not handed to those who will *trample them under their feet*, and Mark's dust is what the twelve do with their own feet when a town will not hear. They do not stay and offer it again. They leave a testimony behind and go.$n$),
(5075,6,14,$n$And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.$n$,'1-kings',21,17,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *That John the Baptist was risen from the dead.* Herod's guilt-haunted confession: the king who beheaded the prophet sees the prophet's pattern reproduced. The Achav (Ahab)-Navot (Naboth) parallel of 1 Kings 21:17 stands behind the conscience.$n$,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* *And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* (Mark 6:14) Herod hears of Yahusha (Jesus) and says John has risen from the dead, and a guilty king's fear shows in it. When Ahab took Naboth's vineyard, *the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite*, and the prophet went down and met him there.$n$),
(5076,6,14,$n$And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.$n$,'mark',9,13,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed* (Mark 9:13) — the King's own naming of Yochanan-as-Eliyahu the chapter before.$n$,$n$Mark 9:13 — *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* *And king Herod heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad:) and he said, That John the Baptist was risen from the dead, and therefore mighty works do shew forth themselves in him.* (Mark 6:14) Herod thinks John has come back from the dead. Later, coming down from the mountain, Yahusha (Jesus) says *Elijah is indeed come* and that they did to him *whatsoever they listed*. Herod is one of those who did it, so what he fears in Yahusha (Jesus) is the man he had beheaded.$n$),
(5077,6,18,$n$For John had said unto Herod, It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife.$n$,'1-kings',21,17,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *It is not lawful for thee to have thy brother's wife.* Yochanan (John) reproves the king the way Eliyahu (Elijah) reproved Achav (Ahab) for Navot's vineyard. The reproof costs his head.$n$,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* *For John had said unto Herod, It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife.* (Mark 6:18) John told Herod he had no right to his brother's wife, and *the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite* to send him to Ahab with a reproof just as plain. Each man spoke the commandment to a king's face, and in each story a queen stood behind the king.$n$),
(5078,6,18,$n$For John had said unto Herod, It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife.$n$,'2-chronicles',24,20,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | The Zecharyah-stoned-between-temple-and-altar pattern (2 Chronicles 24:20-21) — the prophet killed for speaking Torah to power runs straight through to the King's own death.$n$,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *And the Spirit of Elohim (God) came upon Zechariah the son of Jehoiada the priest, which stood above the people, and said unto them, Thus saith Elohim (God), Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper? because ye have forsaken Yahuah (LORD), he hath also forsaken you.* *For John had said unto Herod, It is not lawful for you to have your brother’s wife.* (Mark 6:18) Zechariah son of Jehoiada stood up and asked the people why they transgress *the commandments of Yahuah (LORD)*, and for that the king had him stoned in the court of the house. John held the commandment up to a king in the same way, and it cost him his life.$n$),
(5079,6,30,$n$And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.$n$,'numbers',27,17,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | *Moved with compassion toward them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd.* Mosheh (Moses) used these words at Bamidbar (Numbers) 27:17 — sheep-without-a-shepherd.$n$,$n$Numbers 27:17 — *Which may go out before them, and which may go in before them, and which may lead them out, and which may bring them in; that the congregation of Yahuah (LORD) be not as sheep which have no shepherd.* *And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.* (Mark 6:30) Moses asked for a man to go out before the congregation and come in before them, so they would not be *as sheep which have no shepherd*. Mark has the apostles gathered to Yahusha (Jesus) after their errand, and a few verses on he looks at a crowd and sees that very thing. The one they report to is the shepherd Moses asked for.$n$),
(5080,6,30,$n$And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.$n$,'ezekiel',34,5,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | *Sheep without a shepherd* — the same diagnostic Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 34:5-6 carries against the failed shepherds of Yashar'el.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:5 — *And they were scattered, because there is no shepherd: and they became meat to all the beasts of the field, when they were scattered.* *And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.* (Mark 6:30) Ezekiel says the flock *scattered, because there is no shepherd*, and he blames the shepherds who fed themselves and not the flock. Mark has the apostles come back to the one shepherd after being sent out, and in the verses after he looks at a crowd like that flock and begins to teach them many things.$n$),
(5081,6,30,$n$And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.$n$,'1-kings',22,17,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | Micaiah's vision of *all Yashar'el (Israel) scattered upon the hills, as sheep that have not a shepherd* (1 Kings 22:17) — the same shepherd-language carried forward.$n$,$n$1 Kings 22:17 — *And he said, I saw all Yashar'el (Israel) scattered upon the hills, as sheep that have not a shepherd: and Yahuah (LORD) said, These have no master: let them return every man to his house in peace.* *And the apostles gathered themselves together unto Yahusha (Jesus), and told him all things, both what they had done, and what they had taught.* (Mark 6:30) Micaiah saw all Yashar'el (Israel) *scattered upon the hills, as sheep that have not a shepherd*, and the word was that they should go back each to his house in peace. Mark's crowd, which ran out of every town to meet him, is that flock seen again, and this time a shepherd stands among them.$n$),
(5087,6,37,$n$He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?$n$,'2-kings',4,42,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | Elisha's twenty-loaves multiplication at 2 Kings 4:42-44 — the prophetic pattern of the wilderness-feeding the King fulfills in larger measure.$n$,$n$2 Kings 4:42 — *And there came a man from Baal-shalisha, and brought the man of Elohim (God) bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley, and full ears of corn in the husk thereof. And he said, Give unto the people, that they may eat.* *He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?* (Mark 6:37) Elisha is handed twenty loaves and says *Give unto the people, that they may eat*, and Yahusha (Jesus) says *Give ye them to eat* to men who can only ask about buying bread. In both places the helper counts what he has against the crowd and finds it short, and in both the word is to give it anyway.$n$),
(5088,6,37,$n$He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?$n$,'ezekiel',34,14,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | *I will feed them in a good pasture* (Yechezk'el/Ezekiel 34:14) — the shepherd-feeding-the-flock substance the King walks.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:14 — *I will feed them in a good pasture, and upon the high mountains of Yashar'el (Israel) shall their fold be: there shall they lie in a good fold, and in a fat pasture shall they feed upon the mountains of Yashar'el (Israel).* *He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?* (Mark 6:37) The disciples ask where two hundred pennyworth of bread would come from, and Ezekiel says whose work this is: *I will feed them in a good pasture*, on the mountains of Yashar'el (Israel). Yahuah (the LORD) says he will feed his own scattered flock himself, and the one saying *Give ye them to eat* is the one who does it.$n$),
(5089,6,37,$n$He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?$n$,'ezekiel',34,23,$n$thread:wilderness-feeding-as-messianic-banquet-pre-figuration | *And I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them, even my servant David* (Yechezk'el/Ezekiel 34:23) — the Davidic-shepherd prophecy the wilderness-feeding enacts.$n$,$n$Ezekiel 34:23 — *And I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them, even my servant David; he shall feed them, and he shall be their shepherd.* *He answered and said unto them, Give you them to eat. And they say unto him, Shall we go and buy two hundred pennyworth of bread, and give them to eat?* (Mark 6:37) In Mark the disciples are told to feed the crowd and have only a shopping question to offer. The feeding is done by the shepherd Ezekiel promises, *one shepherd over them*, who *shall feed them, even my servant David*, and the bread goes out through the twelve's own hands.$n$),
(5092,6,48,$n$And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he comes unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them.$n$,'exodus',33,19,$n$thread:walking-on-the-sea-formed-cloud-rider | The *would have passed by them* echoes Yahuah's passing-by Mosheh (Moses) at Shemot (Exodus) 33:19-22 — the theophanic pass-by of the One who treads the sea.$n$,$n$Exodus 33:19 — *And he said, I will make all my goodness pass before thee, and I will proclaim the name of Yahuah (LORD) before thee; and will be gracious to whom I will be gracious, and will shew mercy on whom I will shew mercy.* *And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he comes unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them.* (Mark 6:48) Moses asked to see the glory and was told that Yahuah's (the LORD's) goodness would *pass before thee* while he stood in the rock. In Mark Yahusha (Jesus) comes over the water and *would have passed by them*, in the same words of passing. He is the one the fathers saw go by.$n$),
(5093,6,48,$n$And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he comes unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them.$n$,'1-kings',19,11,$n$thread:walking-on-the-sea-formed-cloud-rider | The theophanic pass-by of Eliyahu (Elijah) at 1 Kings 19:11 — the same pattern carried into the fourth watch on the sea.$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:11 — *And he said, Go forth, and stand upon the mount before Yahuah (LORD). And, behold, Yahuah (LORD) passed by, and a great and strong wind rent the mountains, and brake in pieces the rocks before Yahuah (LORD); but Yahuah (LORD) was not in the wind: and after the wind an earthquake; but Yahuah (LORD) was not in the earthquake:* *And he saw them toiling in rowing; for the wind was contrary unto them: and about the fourth watch of the night he comes unto them, walking upon the sea, and would have passed by them.* (Mark 6:48) Elijah stood on the mountain as *Yahuah (LORD) passed by*, with wind and earthquake and fire coming before the still small voice. The wind in Mark is *contrary unto them*, and in the fourth watch the one who passed by Elijah comes walking through it to men rowing in a boat.$n$),
(5098,6,52,$n$For they considered not the miracle of the loaves: for their heart was hardened.$n$,'isaiah',6,9,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *For they considered not the miracle of the loaves: for their heart was hardened.* The hardening of the heart is the same diagnostic Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 6:9-10 gave the prophet.$n$,$n$Isaiah 6:9 — *And he said, Go, and tell this people, Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not.* *For they considered not the miracle of the loaves: for their heart was hardened.* (Mark 6:52) Isaiah was sent to tell a people *Hear ye indeed, but understand not*. Mark says the like of the twelve themselves, men who had just handed out the loaves and gathered the baskets and still *considered not the miracle of the loaves*. The words that fit the people of Isaiah's day fit the men nearest to Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
(5099,7,3,$n$For the Pharisees, and all the Yahudim (Jews), except they wash their hands oft, eat not, holding the tradition of the elders.$n$,'leviticus',22,1,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *Except they wash their hands oft, eat not, holding the tradition of the elders.* The hand-washing rules are nowhere in the Torah; they are Pharisaic fence-building around the priestly-purity requirements of Vayikra (Leviticus) 22 extended to all the people.$n$,$n$Leviticus 22:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* *For the Pharisees, and all the Yahudim (Jews), except they wash their hands oft, eat not, holding the tradition of the elders.* (Mark 7:3) Leviticus 22 is where Moses' word about the holy things is found, and it is spoken to Aaron and his sons, the priests, about who may eat of what the people hallow. Nothing in it tells every man to wash before he eats bread. The Pharisees took a rule for the priests and laid it on every table as *the tradition of the elders*.$n$),
(5100,7,6,$n$He answered and said unto them, Well has Isaiah prophesied of you hypocrites, as it is written, This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.$n$,'isaiah',29,13,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.* The King quotes Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 29:13 against the tradition-keepers. **Q15 OT-quotation KEEP flag** queued for S184.$n$,$n$Isaiah 29:13 — *Wherefore Yahuah (Lord) said, Forasmuch as this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me, but have removed their heart far from me, and their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men:* *He answered and said unto them, Well has Isaiah prophesied of you hypocrites, as it is written, This people honoureth me with their lips, but their heart is far from me.* (Mark 7:6) Yahusha (Jesus) is not making up a charge. He reads the Pharisees Isaiah's own verse, where *this people* draw near with mouth and lips and their fear of Yahuah (the LORD) is *taught by the precept of men*. Mark's wording and Isaiah's say one thing: the lips are near and *their heart is far from me*.$n$),
(5101,7,8,$n$For laying aside the commandment of Elohim (God), you hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things you do.$n$,'deuteronomy',4,2,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it.* The first blade of the Judaizer-architecture. The Torah's own anti-adding clause stands behind the King's rebuke.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 4:2 — *Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it, that ye may keep the commandments of Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) which I command you.* *For laying aside the commandment of Elohim (God), you hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things you do.* (Mark 7:8) Moses shut this door before any elder tried to open it. *Ye shall not add unto the word*, he said, and the washing of *pots and cups* is a thing added beside the commandment and held as if it weighed the same. Yahusha (Jesus) is holding them to what Moses wrote.$n$),
(5102,7,8,$n$For laying aside the commandment of Elohim (God), you hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things you do.$n$,'deuteronomy',12,32,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *What thing soever I command you, observe to do it: thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it* — the parallel anti-adding clause from Devarim (Deuteronomy) 12:32.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 12:32 — *What thing soever I command you, observe to do it: thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it.* *For laying aside the commandment of Elohim (God), you hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things you do.* (Mark 7:8) Moses says it a second time, right after warning Yashar'el (Israel) not to copy how the nations served their gods: *thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it*. Said twice, so that no elder could claim he was only helping. The washing of pots and cups is something added, and Yahusha (Jesus) names it for what it is, the tradition of men.$n$),
(5103,7,9,$n$And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.$n$,'exodus',20,12,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition.* The load-bearing example: Corban releases sons from honoring parents — voiding the fifth commandment (Shemot/Exodus 20:12) by tradition.$n$,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* *And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.* (Mark 7:9) The commandment he has in mind is one of the Ten: *Honour thy father and thy mother*. A few verses on he shows how they rejected it. A man says Corban, it is a gift, and he is free from helping his parents. The tradition got kept and the fifth commandment did not, which is what rejecting *the commandment of Elohim (God)* looks like here.$n$),
(5104,7,9,$n$And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.$n$,'deuteronomy',5,16,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *Honour thy father and thy mother* — the Devarim (Deuteronomy) 5:16 re-naming of the fifth commandment the Corban tradition voided.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 5:16 — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee; that thy days may be prolonged, and that it may go well with thee, in the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* *And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.* (Mark 7:9) Moses gives the same commandment again to the next generation and adds a reason, *that it may go well with thee*. The honour is meant to keep being done in house after house. The Corban saying, where a son calls what would have helped his parents a gift and so is free of it, stops it at the door of the very house it was given to.$n$),
(5105,7,9,$n$And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.$n$,'1-john',2,3,$n$thread:pharisaic-traditions-make-the-commandment-of-none-effect | *Hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments* — the 1 Yochanan (John) 2:3-4 contrary-to-Torah test; the diagnostic the Pharisaic tradition fails.$n$,$n$1 John 2:3 — *And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments.* *And he said unto them, Full well you reject the commandment of Elohim (God), that you may keep your own tradition.* (Mark 7:9) John says it plainly: *if we keep his commandments* is how we know that we know him. The Pharisees were keeping their own tradition and rejecting the commandment of Elohim (God), and John's next verse speaks of exactly such a man: *He that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him.* (1 John 2:4)$n$),
(5108,7,14,$n$And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:$n$,'jeremiah',17,9,$n$thread:heart-defilement-and-the-mouth-substance | *There is nothing from without a man, that entering into him can defile him.* The defilement at issue is heart-defilement; the diagnostic is Yirmeyahu (Jeremiah) 17:9's *the heart is deceitful above all things.* NOT the abolition of Torah's clean / unclean instruction.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* *And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:* (Mark 7:14) Yahusha (Jesus) turns from the Pharisees to the whole crowd and asks every one of them to hearken, because he is about to say where defilement lives. Jeremiah had already said what the heart is, *deceitful above all things*, and the next verse says who can see into it: *I Yahuah (LORD) search the heart, I try the reins* (Jeremiah 17:10).$n$),
(5109,7,14,$n$And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:$n$,'deuteronomy',30,6,$n$thread:heart-defilement-and-the-mouth-substance | *Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) will circumcise thine heart* (Devarim/Deuteronomy 30:6) — the heart-circumcision the Torah itself prophesied as the answer to defilement.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 30:6 — *And Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) will circumcise thine heart, and the heart of thy seed, to love Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) with all thine heart, and with all thy soul, that thou mayest live.* *And when he had called all the people unto him, he said unto them, Hearken unto me every one of you, and understand:* (Mark 7:14) The cure for a heart like that is not found in a washing bowl. Moses says Yahuah (the LORD) himself *will circumcise thine heart*, in the same passage where he promises to gather the ones driven out to the outmost parts of heaven. The mending of the heart is in the Torah from Moses on.$n$),
(5110,7,19,$n$Because it enters not into his heart, but into the belly, and goes out into the draught, purging all meats?$n$,'leviticus',11,1,$n$thread:oral-tradition-correction-not-torah-supersession | *Purging all meats.* The parenthetical is grammatically the digestive system purging the foods that have already passed through it, not the King declaring unclean animals clean. The Vayikra (Leviticus) 11 categories stand untouched.$n$,$n$Leviticus 11:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and to Aaron, saying unto them,* *Because it enters not into his heart, but into the belly, and goes out into the draught, purging all meats?* (Mark 7:19) Yahusha (Jesus) is speaking about the belly: what a man eats goes *into the belly, and goeth out into the draught*, and never reaches the heart, where he says defilement is made. Which beasts a man may eat is a different question, and Moses had answered it: *These are the beasts which ye shall eat among all the beasts that are on the earth.* (Leviticus 11:2)$n$),
(5111,7,19,$n$Because it enters not into his heart, but into the belly, and goes out into the draught, purging all meats?$n$,'acts',10,14,$n$thread:oral-tradition-correction-not-torah-supersession | Peter's *Not so, Lord; for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean* (Acts 10:14) — confirms the Torah's clean/unclean categories were still operative for the apostolic walk after the resurrection.$n$,$n$Acts 10:14 — *But Peter said, Not so, Yahuah (Lord); for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean.* *Because it enters not into his heart, but into the belly, and goes out into the draught, purging all meats?* (Mark 7:19) The disciples who asked him about this saying heard a word about the belly, and Peter, one of them, is still saying years later, *I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean*. He says it after the resurrection. What he had kept all his life he is still keeping, which is how he understood what Yahusha (Jesus) said at that table.$n$),
(5112,7,21,$n$For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,$n$,'exodus',20,13,$n$thread:heart-defilement-and-the-mouth-substance | *Out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders, thefts, covetousness.* The catalog runs through the second tablet of the Torah (Shemot/Exodus 20:13-17).$n$,$n$Exodus 20:13 — *Thou shalt not kill.* *For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,* (Mark 7:21) What Yahusha (Jesus) says comes out of the heart is a walk down the commandments. *Murders* is *Thou shalt not kill*, and *adulteries* is the commandment that follows it: *Thou shalt not commit adultery.* (Exodus 20:14) The things that defile a man are the Ten broken, which is why he sends the crowd to the heart and not to the washing bowl.$n$),
(5113,7,21,$n$For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,$n$,'jeremiah',17,9,$n$thread:heart-defilement-and-the-mouth-substance | *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked* — the Yirmeyahu (Jeremiah) 17:9 diagnostic.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* *For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,* (Mark 7:21) Jeremiah asks *who can know it?* and Yahusha (Jesus) answers by reading out what the heart holds: *evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders*. The heart that is *desperately wicked* is the one that brings the list out, and no basin of water reaches that.$n$),
(5114,7,21,$n$For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,$n$,'ezekiel',36,26,$n$thread:heart-defilement-and-the-mouth-substance | The answer is the new heart of Yechezk'el (Ezekiel) 36:26 — *a new heart also will I give you.*$n$,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you: and I will take away the stony heart out of your flesh, and I will give you an heart of flesh.* *For from within, out of the heart of men, proceed evil thoughts, adulteries, fornications, murders,* (Mark 7:21) The heart that brings out that list is the one Ezekiel says Yahuah (the LORD) will replace: *A new heart also will I give you*. The next verse says what the new heart is for: *And I will put my spirit within you, and cause you to walk in my statutes, and ye shall keep my judgments, and do them.* (Ezekiel 36:27) The heart is mended so that the commandments get kept.$n$),
(5115,7,24,$n$And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.$n$,'matthew',15,21,$n$thread:canaanite-woman-master | The Markan parallel to the Matthean Canaanite-woman of Matthew 15:21-28. **Red Line #7 individual-mercy contrast case:** mercy granted, daughter healed; absorption into the seed-line not in view here.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:21 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* *And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.* (Mark 7:24) Both gospels put the trip to Tyre and Sidon straight after the argument about unwashen hands. Matthew goes on to the woman crying after him in the open. Mark adds what Matthew leaves out: he went into a house, *would have no man know it*, and could not be hid.$n$),
(5116,7,24,$n$And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.$n$,'matthew',15,24,$n$thread:canaanite-woman-master | *I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* (Matthew 15:24) — the King's stated mission; the bread is for the children of the seed-of-promise, the daughter-of-the-nations receives mercy without being absorbed.$n$,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* *And from thence he arose, and went into the borders of Tyre and Sidon, and entered into an house, and would have no man know it: but he could not be hid.* (Mark 7:24) Mark does not repeat the sentence Matthew keeps, but it explains the woman he is about to meet. Yahusha (Jesus) tells the disciples he is sent *unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel)*, and Mark says of her only that *The woman was a Greek, a Syrophenician by nation* (Mark 7:26). Read side by side, Matthew's sentence tells who she is: one of those lost sheep, a daughter of the scattered house living as a Greek among the nations.$n$),
(5117,7,28,$n$And she answered and said unto him, Yes, Lord: yet the dogs under the table eat of the children’s crumbs.$n$,'matthew',15,27,$n$thread:canaanite-woman-master | *Yet the dogs under the table eat of the children's crumbs.* The woman accepts the children-and-dogs framework the King handed her. Her wisdom is in receiving the framework, not arguing it away. **Q14 NT-Lord vocative flag** queued for S184: render as *Lord* (preserve).$n$,$n$Matthew 15:27 — *And she said, Truth, Adonai (Lord): yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.* *And she answered and said unto him, Yes, Lord: yet the dogs under the table eat of the children’s crumbs.* (Mark 7:28) Both give her the same turn in different words. Matthew's woman says *Truth, Adonai (Lord)* and has the dogs eating from *their masters’ table*. Mark's says Yes and puts the dogs *under the table* eating the *children’s crumbs*. Mark keeps the children in her answer, the same children who were to be filled first, so she takes his order as he gave it and asks only for what falls.$n$),
(5121,8,11,$n$And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.$n$,'matthew',12,38,$n$thread:sign-of-yonah-resurrection-credential | *The Pharisees... seeking of him a sign from heaven.* The Markan compressed version of Matthew 12:38-40 — the only sign given to the sign-seekers is the sign of Yonah (Jonah): three days in the heart of the earth, then risen.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:38 — *Then certain of the scribes and of the Pharisees answered, saying, Master, we would see a sign from thee.* *And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.* (Mark 8:11) Matthew has scribes as well as Pharisees, and they come politely, calling him *Master* and saying *we would see a sign*. Mark strips off the courtesy: they *began to question with him*, and he says what the asking was, *tempting him*. Mark names the test at the start where Matthew lets it be heard in the answer.$n$),
(5122,8,11,$n$And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.$n$,'matthew',16,1,$n$thread:sign-of-yonah-resurrection-credential | The Matthew 16:1-4 parallel sign-seeking exchange.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:1 — *The Pharisees also with the Sadducees came, and tempting desired him that he would shew them a sign from heaven.* *And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.* (Mark 8:11) Matthew adds the Sadducees to the Pharisees, so two parties join in one test, and he too says they came *tempting*. Mark leaves the Sadducees out and keeps the Pharisees, but the thing asked for is the same, *a sign from heaven*, and in both gospels it is asked as a trap.$n$),
(5123,8,11,$n$And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.$n$,'jonah',1,17,$n$thread:sign-of-yonah-resurrection-credential | *Yonah (Jonah) was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights* (Yonah 1:17) — the resurrection-credential Tanakh-substance.$n$,$n$Jonah 1:17 — *Now Yahuah (LORD) had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.* *And the Pharisees came forth, and began to question with him, seeking of him a sign from heaven, tempting him.* (Mark 8:11) Mark gives only the refusal: *There shall no sign be given unto this generation.* (Mark 8:12) Jonah's three days and three nights in the fish are the one thing the scripture holds out to sign-seekers, a man out of sight for three days and then alive. Mark's own words that the Son of Adam must be killed, *and after three days rise again* (Mark 8:31) put the two together.$n$),
(5124,8,15,$n$And he charged them, saying, Take heed, beware of the leaven of the Pharisees, and of the leaven of Herod.$n$,'exodus',12,15,$n$thread:leaven-as-corruption-substance | *Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees, and of the leaven of Herod.* The leaven-as-corruption substance the Torah marks at the Pesach (Passover) preparation (Shemot/Exodus 12:15).$n$,$n$Exodus 12:15 — *Seven days shall ye eat unleavened bread; even the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses: for whosoever eateth leavened bread from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall be cut off from Yashar'el (Israel).* *And he charged them, saying, Take heed, beware of the leaven of the Pharisees, and of the leaven of Herod.* (Mark 8:15) Moses commanded that leaven be put out of the house at Passover, and the soul that ate it was *cut off from Yashar'el (Israel)*. Yahusha (Jesus) uses that picture for the teaching of the Pharisees and of Herod, something small that gets into the whole loaf and has to be cleared out. Mark alone puts Herod in the warning, the king's house beside the Pharisees'.$n$),
(5127,8,17,$n$And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?$n$,'isaiah',6,9,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *Why reason ye, because ye have no bread? perceive ye not yet, neither understand? have ye your heart yet hardened?* The King applies the Yeshayahu (Isaiah) 6:9-10 diagnostic to his own disciples.$n$,$n$Isaiah 6:9 — *And he said, Go, and tell this people, Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not.* *And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?* (Mark 8:17) Yahusha (Jesus) asks the twelve what Isaiah's verse said of the people, *see ye indeed, but perceive not*. He says it to his own disciples, in a boat, over a shortage of bread: *perceive ye not yet, neither understand?* Isaiah's words were sent against a people, and here they land on the men closest to him.$n$),
(5128,8,17,$n$And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?$n$,'jeremiah',5,21,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *Hear now this, O foolish people, and without understanding; which have eyes, and see not* (Yirmeyahu/Jeremiah 5:21).$n$,$n$Jeremiah 5:21 — *Hear now this, O foolish people, and without understanding; which have eyes, and see not; which have ears, and hear not:* *And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?* (Mark 8:17) The next verse of Mark is Jeremiah's sentence turned into a question: *Having eyes, see ye not? and having ears, hear ye not?* (Mark 8:18) Jeremiah spoke it to a *foolish people, and without understanding*. Yahusha (Jesus) says it to men who have just watched two feedings, which is why the hardened heart he names is theirs.$n$),
(5129,8,17,$n$And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?$n$,'ezekiel',12,2,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *They have ears to hear, and hear not: for they are a rebellious house* (Yechezk'el/Ezekiel 12:2).$n$,$n$Ezekiel 12:2 — *Son of Adam, thou dwellest in the midst of a rebellious house, which have eyes to see, and see not; they have ears to hear, and hear not: for they are a rebellious house.* *And when Yahusha (Jesus) knew it, he says unto them, Why reason you, because you have no bread? perceive you not yet, neither understand? have you your heart yet hardened?* (Mark 8:17) Ezekiel was set down in the midst of *a rebellious house* that had eyes to see and did not use them. Yahusha (Jesus) is in a boat with twelve men of that same house, and what he asks them, *have ye your heart yet hardened?*, is Ezekiel's complaint brought inside his own circle. It is said of the disciples, not only of the crowds.$n$),
(5131,8,22,$n$And he comes to Bethsaida; and they bring a blind man unto him, and besought him to touch him.$n$,'isaiah',42,7,$n$thread:messiah-as-torah-affirming-healer | *To open the blind eyes* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 42:7) — the Servant's messianic credential walked out at Bethsaida.$n$,$n$Isaiah 42:7 — *To open the blind eyes, to bring out the prisoners from the prison, and them that sit in darkness out of the prison house.* *And he comes to Bethsaida; and they bring a blind man unto him, and besought him to touch him.* (Mark 8:22) Isaiah says what the one Yahuah (the LORD) sends is for: *To open the blind eyes*. Bethsaida is that work done in front of the disciples who have just been asked whether they see. The man needs two touches before he *saw every man clearly* (Mark 8:25), and the pages around him show the twelve being brought slowly to see too.$n$),
(5132,8,27,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?$n$,'matthew',16,13,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | *Thou art the Messiah (Christ).* Mark's compressed account of the Caesarea Philippi confession; Matthew 16:13-19 gives the rock / keys exchange.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* *And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?* (Mark 8:27) Matthew has him ask it with the title in his mouth, *that I the Son of Adam am*, while Mark's question says only *that I am*. Either way he is asking who men say he is, and Son of Adam is the name he already uses of himself. Mark goes from Peter's confession straight on to the suffering. The rock and the keys are Matthew's and are not here.$n$),
(5133,8,27,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?$n$,'luke',9,18,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | The Lukan parallel of the Caesarea Philippi confession.$n$,$n$Luke 9:18 — *And it came to pass, as he was alone praying, his disciples were with him: and he asked them, saying, Whom say the people that I am?* *And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?* (Mark 8:27) Luke sets the same question somewhere else. Mark has it *by the way* in the towns of Caesarea Philippi, and Luke has a man *alone praying*, with the disciples near him, and the question comes out of the prayer: *Whom say the people that I am?*$n$),
(5134,8,27,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?$n$,'john',6,69,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | *Thou art that Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God)* (Yochanan/John 6:69) — the Johannine parallel.$n$,$n$John 6:69 — *And we believe and are sure that thou art that Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* *And Yahusha (Jesus) went out, and his disciples, into the towns of Cæsarea Philippi: and by the way he asked his disciples, saying unto them, Whom do men say that I am?* (Mark 8:27) Mark gives Peter's answer short: *Thou art the Messiah (Christ).* (Mark 8:29) John has Peter speaking for the others, *we believe and are sure*, and he says more, *the Son of the living Elohim (God)*. It is the same confession in a different place, with the Father's Son named in it.$n$),
(5136,8,31,$n$And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.$n$,'psalms',22,6,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people* (Tehillim/Psalm 22:6-8).$n$,$n$Psalm 22:6 — *But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people.* *And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.* (Mark 8:31) Yahusha (Jesus) says the Son of Adam will suffer much and be *rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes*. The psalm speaks of the same shame in the first person: *a reproach of men, and despised of the people*. The rejection Mark lays out has something written behind it.$n$),
(5137,8,31,$n$And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.$n$,'zechariah',12,10,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *They shall look upon me whom they have pierced* (Zecharyah/Zechariah 12:10).$n$,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *And I will pour upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of supplications: and they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son, and shall be in bitterness for him, as one that is in bitterness for his firstborn.* *And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.* (Mark 8:31) Mark's sentence ends with the Son of Adam killed and risen. Zechariah has a sentence about the people who did it. Yahuah (the LORD) says he will pour out *the spirit of grace and of supplications*, and then *they shall look upon me whom they have pierced*, mourning *as one mourneth for his only son*. The killing is not where the telling stops.$n$),
(5138,8,31,$n$And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.$n$,'matthew',16,21,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | The Matthean parallel of the first passion prediction.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *From that time forth began Yahusha (Jesus) to shew unto his disciples, how that he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised again the third day.* *And he began to teach them, that the Son of Adam must suffer many things, and be rejected of the elders, and of the chief priests, and scribes, and be killed, and after three days rise again.* (Mark 8:31) Matthew marks it with *From that time forth*, says where it ends, *Jerusalem*, and that he would be raised *the third day*. Mark says only that he *began to teach them*, puts the rising *after three days*, and adds that he said it plainly: *And he spake that saying openly.* (Mark 8:32)$n$),
(5142,8,33,$n$But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get you behind me, Satan: for you savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but the things that be of men.$n$,'mark',7,8,$n$thread:pharisaic-sadducean-system-as-judaizer-predecessor | *Get thee behind me, Satan: for thou savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but the things that be of men.* The same diagnostic the King leveled at the Pharisees at 7:8.$n$,$n$Mark 7:8 — *For laying aside the commandment of Elohim (God), ye hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups: and many other such like things ye do.* *But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get you behind me, Satan: for you savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but the things that be of men.* (Mark 8:33) Yahusha (Jesus) charged the Pharisees with laying aside *the commandment of Elohim (God)* for the tradition of men. To Peter he says he savours *the things that be of men* and not the things of Elohim (God). It is the same fault, a mind set on what men say instead of what the Father has said, found in the man at his own side.$n$),
(5143,8,33,$n$But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get you behind me, Satan: for you savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but the things that be of men.$n$,'matthew',16,23,$n$thread:pharisaic-sadducean-system-as-judaizer-predecessor | The Matthean parallel of the same rebuke.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:23 — *But he turned, and said unto Peter, Get thee behind me, Satan: thou art an offence unto me: for thou savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but those that be of men.* *But when he had turned about and looked on his disciples, he rebuked Peter, saying, Get you behind me, Satan: for you savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God), but the things that be of men.* (Mark 8:33) Matthew keeps the same words and adds one of his own, *thou art an offence unto me*. Mark has Yahusha (Jesus) turn first and look *on his disciples* before he speaks, so the rebuke is given where all of them can see it. Matthew says what Peter had become to him in that moment, something to trip over on the road he had just named.$n$),
(5144,8,34,$n$And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'matthew',10,38,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | *Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* The cross is the door home through the curse-system (Galatians 3:13).$n$,$n$Matthew 10:38 — *And he that taketh not his cross, and followeth after me, is not worthy of me.* *And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Mark 8:34) Matthew puts it as a measure, *is not worthy of me*. Mark puts it as a call, *Whosoever will come after me*, and says who was standing there: he *called the people unto him with his disciples also*. The cross is said to the crowd as well as to the twelve, and the words are meant for all of them.$n$),
(5145,8,34,$n$And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'matthew',16,24,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | The Matthean parallel of the take-up-the-cross charge.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* *And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Mark 8:34) The words are nearly the same, but the audience is not. Matthew says Yahusha (Jesus) spoke *unto his disciples*. Mark says he first *called the people unto him with his disciples also*, so the crowd stood there while the cost was named. What Mark adds to Matthew is the crowd.$n$),
(5146,8,34,$n$And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'luke',9,23,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | The Lukan parallel of the take-up-the-cross charge.$n$,$n$Luke 9:23 — *And he said to them all, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.* *And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Mark 8:34) Luke adds a phrase and a word. He says Yahusha (Jesus) spoke *to them all*, which is Mark's crowd along with the disciples, and he puts *daily* after *take up his cross*. In Mark the cross is taken up and followed. In Luke it is carried every day.$n$),
(5147,8,34,$n$And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.$n$,'luke',14,27,$n$thread:take-up-the-cross-discipleship-cost | *Whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple* (Luke 14:27).$n$,$n$Luke 14:27 — *And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.* *And when he had called the people unto him with his disciples also, he said unto them, Whosoever will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* (Mark 8:34) Luke puts the cross on the other side of the line. Where Mark gives the call, *Whosoever will come after me*, Luke gives the loss, *cannot be my disciple*, and says *bear his cross* where Mark says *take up his cross*. A man who will not carry it is not following.$n$),
(5160,9,1,$n$And he said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That there be some of them that stand here, which shall not taste of death, till they have seen the kingdom of Elohim (God) come with power.$n$,'daniel',7,14,$n$thread:appointed-reign-on-the-earth-and-the-priestly-remnant | The kingdom-come-with-power the King names is the appointed-reign breaking-in, witnessed in seed-form in the transfiguration. *And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom* (Daniyel/Daniel 7:14).$n$,$n$Daniel 7:14 — *And there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom, that all people, nations, and languages, should serve him: his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away, and his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed.* *And he said unto them, Verily I say unto you, That there be some of them that stand here, which shall not taste of death, till they have seen the kingdom of Elohim (God) come with power.* (Mark 9:1) Yahusha (Jesus) promises that some standing there will see *the kingdom of Elohim (God) come with power*. Daniel's verse says what that kingdom is: dominion and glory given to the one Daniel saw in his vision, a kingdom for all peoples to serve, and *his kingdom that which shall not be destroyed*.$n$),
(5164,9,4,$n$And there appeared unto them Elijah with Moses: and they were talking with Yahusha (Jesus).$n$,'2-kings',2,11,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *Mosheh (Moses) and Eliyahu (Elijah)... standing now with the One they testified of.* *Eliyahu (Elijah) went up by a whirlwind into heaven* (2 Kings 2:11-12).$n$,$n$2 Kings 2:11 — *And it came to pass, as they still went on, and talked, that, behold, there appeared a chariot of fire, and horses of fire, and parted them both asunder; and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.* *And there appeared unto them Elijah with Moses: and they were talking with Yahusha (Jesus).* (Mark 9:4) Elijah stands on the mountain talking with Yahusha (Jesus), and Second Kings tells how he left the earth: *a chariot of fire, and horses of fire* parted him from Elisha, and *Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven*. The man carried up in the whirlwind is the one who now appears beside Moses.$n$),
(5165,9,4,$n$And there appeared unto them Elijah with Moses: and they were talking with Yahusha (Jesus).$n$,'deuteronomy',34,5,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | Mosheh (Moses)'s death by Yahuah's mouth at Devarim (Deuteronomy) 34:5-6 — the lawgiver translated, now standing on the mountain.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 34:5 — *So Moses the servant of Yahuah (LORD) died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of Yahuah (LORD).* *And there appeared unto them Elijah with Moses: and they were talking with Yahusha (Jesus).* (Mark 9:4) Moses is the other one on the mountain, and Deuteronomy says how his life ended: he *died there in the land of Moab*, the servant of Yahuah (the LORD), by his word. The lawgiver and the prophet who went up in the whirlwind now stand together talking with the one both of them wrote toward.$n$),
(5174,9,13,$n$But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.$n$,'1-kings',19,2,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed.* The King identifies Yochanan (John) as the Eliyahu-pattern prophet sent before the dreadful day, killed by the same Yezevel (Jezebel)-spirit that hunted the first (1 Kings 19:2, 10).$n$,$n$1 Kings 19:2 — *Then Jezebel sent a messenger unto Elijah, saying, So let the gods do to me, and more also, if I make not thy life as the life of one of them by to morrow about this time.* *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* (Mark 9:13) Yahusha (Jesus) says of John, *they have done unto him whatsoever they listed*. Jezebel's threat to Elijah is the same hatred toward the same kind of prophet, a message saying she will make his life *as the life of one of them* by tomorrow. The man who comes in Elijah's power meets what Elijah was threatened with.$n$),
(5175,9,13,$n$But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.$n$,'malachi',4,5,$n$thread:yochanan-as-eliyahu-pattern-prophet | *Behold, I will send you Eliyahu (Elijah) the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (the LORD)* (Mal'aki/Malachi 4:5).$n$,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* (Mark 9:13) Malachi promised that Yahuah (the LORD) would send Elijah the prophet before *the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD)*. Yahusha (Jesus) says the one promised *is indeed come*, and means John, and adds that what was done to him was *written of him* too.$n$),
(5176,9,13,$n$But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.$n$,'2-chronicles',24,20,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | The Zecharyah-stoned-in-the-temple-courts pattern (2 Chronicles 24:20-22).$n$,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *And the Spirit of Elohim (God) came upon Zechariah the son of Jehoiada the priest, which stood above the people, and said unto them, Thus saith Elohim (God), Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper? because ye have forsaken Yahuah (LORD), he hath also forsaken you.* *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* (Mark 9:13) What they did to John *whatsoever they listed* was done before to Zechariah the son of Jehoiada. The Spirit of Elohim (God) came on him and he stood above the people and asked, *Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD)*? The prophet who set the commandments before a people that had left them was the one they killed.$n$),
(5177,9,13,$n$But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.$n$,'matthew',23,35,$n$thread:eliyahu-pattern-prophet-martyrdom | *Even unto the blood of Zecharyah (Zechariah), the son of Berechyah, whom ye slew between the temple and the altar* (Matthew 23:35).$n$,$n$Matthew 23:35 — *That upon you may come all the righteous blood shed upon the earth, from the blood of righteous Abel unto the blood of Zechariah son of Barachias, whom ye slew between the temple and the altar.* *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* (Mark 9:13) Yahusha (Jesus) counts the killing of the prophets in a line from Abel to a man slain *between the temple and the altar*. John, done to *whatsoever they listed*, stands in that line. Chronicles gives the dead prophet's father as Jehoiada where Matthew writes Barachias.$n$),
(5172,9,9,$n$And as they came down from the mountain, he charged them that they should tell no man what things they had seen, till the Son of Adam were risen from the dead.$n$,'isaiah',53,3,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *The Son of Adam was risen from the dead.* The charge binds the transfiguration's glory to the cross's suffering — *despised and rejected of men* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 53:3, 10).$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:3 — *He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not.* *And as they came down from the mountain, he charged them that they should tell no man what things they had seen, till the Son of Adam were risen from the dead.* (Mark 9:9) Right off the mountain, with the glory still on them, Yahusha (Jesus) tells them to say nothing *till the Son of Adam were risen from the dead*. Isaiah had already drawn what lies between the glory and the rising: a man *despised and rejected of men*, who knew sorrow and grief.$n$),
(5181,9,31,$n$For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day.$n$,'isaiah',53,8,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men.* The second passion prediction — *he was taken from prison and from judgment* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 53:8).$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:8 — *He was taken from prison and from judgment: and who shall declare his generation? for he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken.* *For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day.* (Mark 9:31) *The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men*, he teaches the twelve, and Isaiah has the shape of it: *taken from prison and from judgment*, *cut off out of the land of the living*. The reason Isaiah gives is not his own doing: *for the transgression of my people was he stricken*.$n$),
(5182,9,31,$n$For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day.$n$,'zechariah',13,7,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *Smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered* (Zecharyah/Zechariah 13:7).$n$,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *Awake, O sword, against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts): smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* *For he taught his disciples, and said unto them, The Son of Adam is delivered into the hands of men, and they shall kill him; and after that he is killed, he shall rise the third day.* (Mark 9:31) Yahusha (Jesus) teaches that *they shall kill him*, and that he shall rise the third day. Zechariah has Yahuah (the LORD) call the sword *against my shepherd*, and what follows the smiting is the flock: *the sheep shall be scattered*.$n$),
(5185,9,37,$n$Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receives me: and whosoever shall receive me, receives not me, but him that sent me.$n$,'matthew',12,50,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *Receiving one of these little children in the King's name is receiving the King.* *Whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother* (Matthew 12:50).$n$,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* *Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receives me: and whosoever shall receive me, receives not me, but him that sent me.* (Mark 9:37) To receive a child *in my name*, says Yahusha (Jesus), is to receive him, and to receive him is to receive the Father who sent him. Matthew gives the household the same test: whoever does *the will of my Father which is in heaven* is brother, sister and mother. The family is known by the doing.$n$),
(5186,9,37,$n$Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receives me: and whosoever shall receive me, receives not me, but him that sent me.$n$,'john',13,20,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *He that receiveth whomsoever I send receiveth me* (Yochanan/John 13:20).$n$,$n$John 13:20 — *Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that receiveth whomsoever I send receiveth me; and he that receiveth me receiveth him that sent me.* *Whosoever shall receive one of such children in my name, receives me: and whosoever shall receive me, receives not me, but him that sent me.* (Mark 9:37) The same chain runs through both books: the one who receives the one sent receives the sender. Mark has it at a child, *receiveth me: and whosoever shall receive me, receiveth not me, but him that sent me*. John has Yahusha (Jesus) say it of those he sends out, so a sent one's welcome rests on the same word as a child's.$n$),
(5196,10,6,$n$But from the beginning of the creation Elohim (God) made them male and female.$n$,'genesis',2,24,$n$thread:the-seed-of-promise-is-singular-paternal | *They twain shall be one flesh.* The two-become-one of Bereshit (Genesis) 2:24 is the marriage-mechanism the framework reads forward as the seed-of-promise architecture.$n$,$n$Genesis 2:24 — *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* *But from the beginning of the creation Elohim (God) made them male and female.* (Mark 10:6) Yahusha (Jesus) goes back past Moses' bill of divorcement to the beginning, where Elohim (God) *made them male and female*. Genesis gives the next sentence of that beginning: a man leaves his father and mother, cleaves to his wife, and *they shall be one flesh*. Two made one, the wife taken into her husband's house, is the pattern from the start.$n$),
(5197,10,14,$n$But when Yahusha (Jesus) saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God).$n$,'matthew',18,3,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *Of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God).* The kingdom is entered as the gift it is, not as the rank one climbs to (Matthew 18:3-4).$n$,$n$Matthew 18:3 — *And said, Verily I say unto you, Except ye be converted, and become as little children, ye shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.* *But when Yahusha (Jesus) saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Mark 10:14) *Of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God)*, he says over the children brought to him. Matthew has him say it to the disciples who asked who was greatest: *Except ye be converted, and become as little children*, or they will not enter. The kingdom is entered as a child takes a gift, not as a rank climbed to.$n$),
(5198,10,14,$n$But when Yahusha (Jesus) saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God).$n$,'psalms',131,1,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *Yahuah (LORD), my heart is not haughty* (Tehillim/Psalm 131:1-2) — the kingdom-posture.$n$,$n$Psalm 131:1 — *Yahuah (LORD), my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty: neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me.* *But when Yahusha (Jesus) saw it, he was much displeased, and said unto them, Suffer the little children to come unto me, and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Mark 10:14) The kingdom belongs to those who come small, and the psalm is a man who has come that way: *my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty*, and no reaching after *things too high for me*. That is the child Yahusha (Jesus) set before them, one who takes what is given and does not grasp.$n$),
(5203,10,28,$n$Then Peter began to say unto him, Lo, we have left all, and have followed you.$n$,'matthew',12,50,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | *The hundredfold-now — houses, brethren, sisters, mothers, children, lands.* The kingdom-family is gathered around the doing of the Father's will (Matthew 12:50).$n$,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* *Then Peter began to say unto him, Lo, we have left all, and have followed you.* (Mark 10:28) Peter counts what the twelve have left behind: *we have left all, and have followed thee*. Yahusha (Jesus) answers with brethren and sisters and mothers and children, a whole household, and Matthew shows how that household is known: whoever does *the will of my Father which is in heaven* is brother, sister and mother.$n$),
(5204,10,28,$n$Then Peter began to say unto him, Lo, we have left all, and have followed you.$n$,'mark',3,35,$n$thread:kinship-redefinition-kingdom-family | The Mark 3:35 architecture repeated here in the hundredfold-now register.$n$,$n$Mark 3:35 — *For whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother.* *Then Peter began to say unto him, Lo, we have left all, and have followed you.* (Mark 10:28) Mark has already said who the family is, and Peter's *we have left all* is the answer at the other end of it. Those sitting round him were told that whoever does *the will of Elohim (God)* is his brother and sister and mother. The hundredfold household of brethren and mothers gathers round that same will.$n$),
(5205,10,31,$n$But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.$n$,'matthew',22,14,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *Many that are first shall be last; and the last first.* *Many are called, but few are chosen* (Matthew 22:14).$n$,$n$Matthew 22:14 — *For many are called, but few are chosen.* *But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.* (Mark 10:31) *Many that are first shall be last; and the last first*, he says, and Matthew's wedding parable closes with its twin: *many are called, but few are chosen*. Both say that the order men reckon among themselves is not the order of the Father's choosing, and that the choosing is his.$n$),
(5206,10,31,$n$But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.$n$,'matthew',19,30,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | The Matthean parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 19:30 — *But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first.* *But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.* (Mark 10:31) Matthew sets this saying after the promise that the twelve shall sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel). Mark gives it after the hundredfold with persecutions, and the turning over of first and last stands beside the cost of following.$n$),
(5207,10,31,$n$But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.$n$,'luke',13,30,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | The Lukan parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 13:30 — *And, behold, there are last which shall be first, and there are first which shall be last.* *But many that are first shall be last; and the last first.* (Mark 10:31) Luke sets the same words at a shut door, with Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and all the prophets in the kingdom of Elohim (God) and some thrust out. Those who come from the east and west and north and south sit down beside the fathers, and last and first change places there.$n$),
(5208,10,32,$n$And they were in the way going up to Jerusalem; and Yahusha (Jesus) went before them: and they were amazed; and as they followed, they were afraid. And he took again the twelve, and began to tell them what things should happen unto him,$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | *The third passion prediction is the most explicit.* *He is brought as a lamb to the slaughter* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 53:7).$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *And they were in the way going up to Jerusalem; and Yahusha (Jesus) went before them: and they were amazed; and as they followed, they were afraid. And he took again the twelve, and began to tell them what things should happen unto him,* (Mark 10:32) On the road up to Jerusalem *Yahusha (Jesus) went before them*, the twelve following afraid, and he began to tell them *what things should happen unto him*. Isaiah had drawn the one who walks to it: *brought as a lamb to the slaughter*, and *he openeth not his mouth*.$n$),
(5209,10,32,$n$And they were in the way going up to Jerusalem; and Yahusha (Jesus) went before them: and they were amazed; and as they followed, they were afraid. And he took again the twelve, and began to tell them what things should happen unto him,$n$,'psalms',22,6,$n$thread:first-passion-prediction-and-the-must-of-the-passion | The Yerushalayim-arrival is the destination he has been walking toward since Caesarea Philippi (Tehillim/Psalm 22:6-8).$n$,$n$Psalm 22:6 — *But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people.* *And they were in the way going up to Jerusalem; and Yahusha (Jesus) went before them: and they were amazed; and as they followed, they were afraid. And he took again the twelve, and began to tell them what things should happen unto him,* (Mark 10:32) He walks ahead of the afraid twelve toward Jerusalem, and what he is about to tell them is met in the psalm by one who says *I am a worm, and no man*, a reproach *despised of the people*. The one who goes before them is going toward that.$n$),
(5214,10,46,$n$And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.$n$,'jeremiah',23,5,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | *Thou Son of David, have mercy on me.* *I will raise unto David a righteous Branch* (Yirmeyahu/Jeremiah 23:5). **Q14 NT-Lord flag** at 10:51 queued for S184.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 23:5 — *Behold, the days come, saith Yahuah (LORD), that I will raise unto David a righteous Branch, and a King shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.* *And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.* (Mark 10:46) The blind man by the road at Jericho will cry out to him as the Son of David, and Jeremiah gives what that name carries: Yahuah (the LORD) *will raise unto David a righteous Branch*, a king who *shall execute judgment and justice in the earth*. A beggar names the Branch before the crowd does.$n$),
(5215,10,46,$n$And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.$n$,'2-samuel',7,12,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | 2 Sh'mu'el (Samuel) 7:12-13's Davidic seed-promise.$n$,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *And when thy days be fulfilled, and thou shalt sleep with thy fathers, I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.* *And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.* (Mark 10:46) The blind man cries to the Son of David, and Nathan's word to David is where the name comes from: *I will set up thy seed after thee*, and *I will establish his kingdom*. The beggar by the road is holding Yahusha (Jesus) to that promise.$n$),
(5216,10,46,$n$And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.$n$,'isaiah',11,1,$n$thread:son-of-david-messianic-recognition-by-marginalized | *A rod out of the stem of Yishai (Jesse)* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 11:1).$n$,$n$Isaiah 11:1 — *And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:* *And they came to Jericho: and as he went out of Jericho with his disciples and a great number of people, blind Bartimæus, the son of Timæus, sat by the highway side begging.* (Mark 10:46) Isaiah's *rod out of the stem of Jesse* is the same hope in another picture: David's father cut down to a stem and a *Branch* growing out of his roots. When the blind man cries out to the Son of David, he is naming that Branch.$n$),
(5217,10,52,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Go your way; your faith has made you whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way.$n$,'john',10,27,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | *Thy faith hath made thee whole.* Bartimaeus was a son-of-Adam from before the foundation; his hearing was the revealing, not the creating. *My sheep hear my voice* (Yochanan/John 10:27).$n$,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Go your way; your faith has made you whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way.* (Mark 10:52) *Thy faith hath made thee whole*, says Yahusha (Jesus), and the man *followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way*. John gives the shape of it: *My sheep hear my voice*, and *they follow me*. Bartimaeus kept crying when the crowd told him to hush; hearing showed what he was, it did not make him one.$n$),
(5218,10,52,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Go your way; your faith has made you whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way.$n$,'revelation',13,8,$n$thread:parables-of-the-kingdom-and-the-cause-and-effect-of-hearing | Revelation 13:8's *names written before the foundation* — the hearing reveals the prior election.$n$,$n$Revelation 13:8 — *And all that dwell upon the earth shall worship him, whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, Go your way; your faith has made you whole. And immediately he received his sight, and followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way.* (Mark 10:52) Revelation's own verse speaks of names *not written in the book of life*, of the Lamb *slain from the foundation of the world*, which puts that book before the foundation, older than any man's hearing. Bartimaeus cried out and *followed Yahusha (Jesus) in the way*, showing whose he was.$n$),
(5230,11,25,$n$And when you stand praying, forgive, if you have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.$n$,'leviticus',19,18,$n$thread:love-brings-torah-to-pass | *Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself* (Vayikra/Leviticus 19:18). The Lord's-prayer architecture is not a transaction but a heart-shape — heart-Torah walked.$n$,$n$Leviticus 19:18 — *Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people, but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am Yahuah (LORD).* *And when you stand praying, forgive, if you have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.* (Mark 11:25) *When ye stand praying, forgive*, he says, and Leviticus has the same command in the commandment itself: *Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge*, and *thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself*. Forgiving at the hour of prayer is that Torah kept, the grudge let go as the man asks to be forgiven.$n$),
(5231,11,25,$n$And when you stand praying, forgive, if you have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.$n$,'matthew',6,14,$n$thread:love-brings-torah-to-pass | *For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you* (Matthew 6:14-15).$n$,$n$Matthew 6:14 — *For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you:* *And when you stand praying, forgive, if you have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses.* (Mark 11:25) Matthew puts this right beneath the pattern of prayer: *if ye forgive men their trespasses*, the heavenly Father will forgive too. Mark sets it at the end of the word on prayer after the fig tree, as the thing to do *when ye stand praying*, with *if ye have ought against any* spoken to the man already on his feet before the Father.$n$),
(5232,11,27,$n$And they come again to Jerusalem: and as he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders,$n$,'matthew',16,13,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | The chief priests' authority-question is the same authority-architecture Caesarea Philippi named.$n$,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* *And they come again to Jerusalem: and as he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders,* (Mark 11:27) At Caesarea Philippi Yahusha (Jesus) asked *Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* and got only what men guessed. In the temple *the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders* ask by what authority he does these things, and when he asks about John's baptism they will not say whether it was from heaven.$n$),
(5233,11,27,$n$And they come again to Jerusalem: and as he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders,$n$,'matthew',21,23,$n$thread:caesarea-philippi-confession-the-rock-the-keys-the-assembly | The Matthean parallel of the temple-authority exchange.$n$,$n$Matthew 21:23 — *And when he was come into the temple, the chief priests and the elders of the people came unto him as he was teaching, and said, By what authority doest thou these things? and who gave thee this authority?* *And they come again to Jerusalem: and as he was walking in the temple, there come to him the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders,* (Mark 11:27) Mark names three groups, *the chief priests, and the scribes, and the elders*, where Matthew names *the chief priests and the elders of the people*, and Mark has him *walking in the temple* where Matthew has him teaching. The question is the same, and so is his answer about John's baptism.$n$),
(5236,12,1,$n$And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.$n$,'romans',11,5,$n$thread:sovereign-election-and-the-remnant-of-yashar-el | *Yashar'el (Israel) hath not obtained that which he seeketh for; but the election hath obtained it* (Romans 11:5-7). The seed of Yashar'el is not displaced by the nations; the unfruitful husbandmen are sifted out, and the priestly remnant inherits.$n$,$n$Romans 11:5 — *Even so then at this present time also there is a remnant according to the election of grace.* *And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.* (Mark 12:1) Yahusha (Jesus) opens with a vineyard let out to husbandmen who will not render the owner his fruit. Paul speaks of the same house in his day: *there is a remnant according to the election of grace*, a chosen part of Yashar'el (Israel) standing where the whole did not.$n$),
(5237,12,1,$n$And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.$n$,'isaiah',10,21,$n$thread:sovereign-election-and-the-remnant-of-yashar-el | *The remnant shall return, even the remnant of Ya'aqov (Jacob)* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 10:21-22).$n$,$n$Isaiah 10:21 — *The remnant shall return, even the remnant of Jacob, unto El Gibbor (the mighty God).* *And he began to speak unto them by parables. A certain man planted a vineyard, and set an hedge about it, and digged a place for the winefat, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country.* (Mark 12:1) *The remnant shall return, even the remnant of Jacob*, says Isaiah, and names the chosen part as Jacob's own house. Whatever comes to the husbandmen in the parable, the remnant that survives it is of Jacob and returns unto El Gibbor (the mighty God).$n$),
(5244,12,26,$n$And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?$n$,'exodus',3,6,$n$thread:natural-branches-asleep-grafted-again | *I am the Elohim (God) of Avraham (Abraham), and the Elohim (God) of Yitschaq (Isaac), and the Elohim (God) of Ya'aqov (Jacob).* The patriarchs are not annihilated but asleep, awaiting the resurrection.$n$,$n$Exodus 3:6 — *Moreover he said, I am the Elohim (God) of thy father, the Elohim (God) of Abraham, the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob. And Moses hid his face; for he was afraid to look upon Elohim (God).* *And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?* (Mark 12:26) Yahusha (Jesus) sends the Sadducees to the bush, where Elohim (God) names himself *the Elohim (God) of Abraham*, of Isaac, and of Jacob. The fathers had been in the ground for generations when he said it, and Yahusha (Jesus) draws the plain conclusion: he is the Elohim (God) of the living, so the fathers are not gone.$n$),
(5245,12,26,$n$And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?$n$,'daniel',12,2,$n$thread:natural-branches-asleep-grafted-again | *And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake* (Daniyel/Daniel 12:2).$n$,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt.* *And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?* (Mark 12:26) If the fathers are the living's Elohim (God) in his sense, Daniel says where they are: those who *sleep in the dust of the earth* shall *awake*. Asleep in the dust is how Daniel speaks of the dead, and awake is what is coming to them, in Daniel's own words, the same rising the Sadducees denied.$n$),
(5246,12,26,$n$And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?$n$,'hebrews',11,13,$n$thread:natural-branches-asleep-grafted-again | Hebrews 11:13-16, 39-40's witness that the patriarchs *all died in faith, not having received the promises*.$n$,$n$Hebrews 11:13 — *These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off, and were persuaded of them, and embraced them, and confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth.* *And as touching the dead, that they rise: have you not read in the book of Moses, how in the bush Elohim (God) spake unto him, saying, I am the Elohim (God) of Abraham, and the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob?* (Mark 12:26) *These all died in faith, not having received the promises*, Hebrews says of the fathers, the same fathers Yahusha (Jesus) puts in the bush. They embraced promises they saw only *afar off*, so the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob is the Elohim (God) of men who sleep still waiting on what was promised.$n$),
(5252,12,32,$n$And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:$n$,'hosea',6,6,$n$thread:mercy-not-sacrifice-hosea-6-6-citation | *To love him with all the heart... is more than all whole burnt offerings and sacrifices.* The scribe's recognition walks Hoshea (Hosea) 6:6.$n$,$n$Hosea 6:6 — *For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of Elohim (God) more than burnt offerings.* *And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:* (Mark 12:32) The scribe says that to love him with all the heart and the neighbour as himself is more than all the offerings. Hosea gave it in the voice of Yahuah (the LORD): *I desired mercy, and not sacrifice*, and the knowledge of Elohim (God) above burnt offerings. The scribe is repeating what the prophets already said.$n$),
(5253,12,32,$n$And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:$n$,'1-samuel',15,22,$n$thread:mercy-not-sacrifice-hosea-6-6-citation | *To obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams* (1 Sh'mu'el/Samuel 15:22).$n$,$n$1 Samuel 15:22 — *And Samuel said, Hath Yahuah (LORD) as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of Yahuah (LORD)? Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams.* *And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:* (Mark 12:32) Samuel asked whether Yahuah (the LORD) delights in offerings as much as in being obeyed, and answered, *to obey is better than sacrifice*. The scribe's *is more than all whole burnt offerings and sacrifices* (Mark 12:33) is that same ranking, with the obeying of the voice put first.$n$),
(5254,12,32,$n$And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:$n$,'micah',6,6,$n$thread:mercy-not-sacrifice-hosea-6-6-citation | Mikhah (Micah) 6:6-8's *what doth Yahuah (the LORD) require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy.*$n$,$n$Micah 6:6 — *Wherewith shall I come before Yahuah (LORD), and bow myself before the high Elohim (God)? shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old?* *And the scribe said unto him, Well, Master, you have said the truth: for there is one Elohim (God); and there is none other but he:* (Mark 12:32) Micah asks the question for the scribe: *Wherewith shall I come before Yahuah (LORD)*, with burnt offerings and year-old calves? And answers it as the scribe has just answered Yahusha (Jesus): *to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy Elohim (God)* (Micah 6:8).$n$),
(5263,13,6,$n$For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.$n$,'matthew',24,5,$n$thread:another-gospel-as-judaizer-flesh-credential | *Many shall come in my name... and shall deceive many.* The lie wears the Messiah's name; the test is the same contrary-to-Torah question that exposes the lie in every age.$n$,$n$Matthew 24:5 — *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* (Mark 13:6) Both gospels give the warning in the same words, but Mark sets it on the Mount of Olives with Peter and James and John and Andrew asking privately what the sign would be. Matthew has the disciples ask about his coming and the end of the world. Either way, the first thing Yahusha (Jesus) says is that deceivers will use his name.$n$),
(5264,13,6,$n$For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.$n$,'luke',21,8,$n$thread:another-gospel-as-judaizer-flesh-credential | The Lukan parallel of the warning.$n$,$n$Luke 21:8 — *And he said, Take heed that ye be not deceived: for many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and the time draweth near: go ye not therefore after them.* *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* (Mark 13:6) Luke carries the same warning and adds two things Mark does not: the deceivers will say *the time draweth near*, and Yahusha (Jesus) gives the plain order *go ye not therefore after them*. He does not only say such men will come; he tells his hearers not to follow.$n$),
(5265,13,6,$n$For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.$n$,'2-corinthians',11,4,$n$thread:another-gospel-as-judaizer-flesh-credential | *Another Yahusha (Jesus), whom we have not preached... another gospel* (2 Corinthians 11:4).$n$,$n$2 Corinthians 11:4 — *For if he that cometh preacheth another Yahusha (Jesus), whom we have not preached, or if ye receive another spirit, which ye have not received, or another gospel, which ye have not accepted, ye might well bear with him.* *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* (Mark 13:6) Paul found the same danger at Corinth: a man comes preaching *another Yahusha (Jesus)*, bringing another spirit and another gospel, and the people are ready to put up with him. What Yahusha (Jesus) said would come in his name was already walking into a congregation.$n$),
(5266,13,6,$n$For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.$n$,'galatians',1,6,$n$thread:another-gospel-as-judaizer-flesh-credential | *Removed... unto another gospel* (Galatians 1:6).$n$,$n$Galatians 1:6 — *I marvel that ye are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Messiah (Christ) unto another gospel:* *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* (Mark 13:6) Paul is amazed at how quickly people were *so soon removed* from the one who called them *unto another gospel*. The warning Yahusha (Jesus) gave on the Mount of Olives was not about a far-off day; Paul saw it already happening in the assemblies of Galatia.$n$),
(5278,13,22,$n$For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.$n$,'deuteronomy',13,1,$n$thread:i-know-thy-works-and-names-blotted-out | *False Christs and false prophets... shew signs and wonders.* The signs and wonders do not vouch for the source (Devarim/Deuteronomy 13:1-5); the contrary-to-Torah test does.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 13:1 — *If there arise among you a prophet, or a dreamer of dreams, and giveth thee a sign or a wonder,* *For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.* (Mark 13:22) Moses had said it long before: a prophet may give a sign or a wonder and still not be from Yahuah (the LORD). The very next words show what the test is, *Let us go after other gods, which thou hast not known* (Deuteronomy 13:2). Yahusha (Jesus) is sending his disciples back to that test.$n$),
(5279,13,22,$n$For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.$n$,'revelation',13,13,$n$thread:i-know-thy-works-and-names-blotted-out | Revelation 13:13-14's signs-of-the-second-beast.$n$,$n$Revelation 13:13 — *And he doeth great wonders, so that he maketh fire come down from heaven on the earth in the sight of men,* *For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.* (Mark 13:22) John saw a beast that does *great wonders* and brings *fire come down from heaven* in the sight of men. Mark gives the warning beforehand; Revelation shows what such wonders look like when they are staged for everyone to watch.$n$),
(5280,13,22,$n$For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.$n$,'2-thessalonians',2,9,$n$thread:i-know-thy-works-and-names-blotted-out | 2 Thessalonians 2:9's *all power and signs and lying wonders*.$n$,$n$2 Thessalonians 2:9 — *Even him, whose coming is after the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders,* *For false Christs and false prophets shall rise, and shall shew signs and wonders, to seduce, if it were possible, even the elect.* (Mark 13:22) Paul says where such wonders come from: the coming of the one he describes is *after the working of Satan*, with *all power and signs and lying wonders*. That is why Yahusha (Jesus) can say signs and wonders might seduce even the elect. They can be lying wonders, power and all.$n$),
(5281,13,24,$n$But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,$n$,'joel',2,10,$n$thread:trumpets-and-the-day-of-yahuah | *The sun darkened, the moon failing her light, the stars falling.* The day-of-Yahuah cosmic-signs (Yo'el/Joel 2:10, 30-31).$n$,$n$Joel 2:10 — *The earth shall quake before them; the heavens shall tremble: the sun and the moon shall be dark, and the stars shall withdraw their shining:* *But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,* (Mark 13:24) Joel sees the lights go out as the army of Yahuah (the LORD) comes, and he names the time: *the day of Yahuah (LORD) is great and very terrible* (Joel 2:11). When Yahusha (Jesus) says the sun and moon will be darkened, he is pointing at that day of the prophets, not at a new sign.$n$),
(5282,13,24,$n$But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,$n$,'isaiah',13,10,$n$thread:trumpets-and-the-day-of-yahuah | *The stars of heaven and the constellations thereof shall not give their light* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 13:10).$n$,$n$Isaiah 13:10 — *For the stars of heaven and the constellations thereof shall not give their light: the sun shall be darkened in his going forth, and the moon shall not cause her light to shine.* *But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,* (Mark 13:24) Isaiah adds the stars and their constellations to the darkened sun and moon, and he sets all of it in a day of judgment: *Behold, the day of Yahuah (LORD) cometh, cruel both with wrath and fierce anger* (Isaiah 13:9). Yahusha (Jesus) says *the moon shall not give her light* and speaks in the same line of the prophet.$n$),
(5283,13,24,$n$But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,$n$,'isaiah',34,4,$n$thread:trumpets-and-the-day-of-yahuah | *All the host of heaven shall be dissolved* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 34:4).$n$,$n$Isaiah 34:4 — *And all the host of heaven shall be dissolved, and the heavens shall be rolled together as a scroll: and all their host shall fall down, as the leaf falleth off from the vine, and as a falling fig from the fig tree.* *But in those days, after that tribulation, the sun shall be darkened, and the moon shall not give her light,* (Mark 13:24) In Isaiah the darkness goes further: the host of heaven *shall be dissolved* and the heavens *rolled together as a scroll*, falling like a leaf from a vine. It comes when *the indignation of Yahuah (LORD) is upon all nations* (Isaiah 34:2), so Yahusha (Jesus) is using a picture of judgment the prophet already drew.$n$),
(5284,13,26,$n$And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.$n$,'daniel',7,13,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | *The Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.* Direct-attribution cloud-rider line (no kaph). The Formed cloud-rider whom Daniyel (Daniel) 7:13-14 saw in vision is the King naming his own return.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *I saw in the night visions, and, behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days, and they brought him near before him.* *And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.* (Mark 13:26) Daniel saw *one like the Son of Adam* come *with the clouds of heaven* to the Ancient of days, and Daniel's next verse says what was given him: *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away* (Daniel 7:14). Yahusha (Jesus) says it outright in Mark: the one they will see coming is the one Daniel saw.$n$),
(5285,13,26,$n$And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.$n$,'matthew',24,30,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | The Matthean parallel of the cloud-coming.$n$,$n$Matthew 24:30 — *And then shall appear the sign of the Son of Adam in heaven: and then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.* *And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.* (Mark 13:26) Matthew has the same coming and adds two things Mark does not: *the sign of the Son of Adam in heaven* appears first, and *all the tribes of the earth mourn* when they see him. Mark says only that they will see him; Matthew tells what the seeing does to the tribes.$n$),
(5286,13,26,$n$And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.$n$,'revelation',1,7,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | *Behold, he cometh with clouds; and every eye shall see him* (Revelation 1:7).$n$,$n$Revelation 1:7 — *Behold, he cometh with clouds; and every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him. Even so, Amen.* *And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.* (Mark 13:26) John sees the same coming: *he cometh with clouds*, and *every eye shall see him*, even *they also which pierced him*. Where Mark says they shall see, Revelation says who is among the seers and that *all kindreds of the earth shall wail* because of him.$n$),
(5287,13,26,$n$And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.$n$,'zechariah',12,10,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | *They shall look upon me whom they have pierced* (Zecharyah/Zechariah 12:10).$n$,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *And I will pour upon the house of David, and upon the inhabitants of Jerusalem, the spirit of grace and of supplications: and they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son, and shall be in bitterness for him, as one that is in bitterness for his firstborn.* *And then shall they see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds with great power and glory.* (Mark 13:26) Here the one who speaks says *they shall look upon me whom they have pierced*, and the mourning that follows is for him, like the grief for *his only son*. Mark says they will see the Son of Adam coming; Zechariah says what that seeing is, a looking upon the one who was pierced.$n$),
(5288,13,27,$n$And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.$n$,'matthew',13,41,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | *The angels gather the elect from the four winds.* *The Son of Adam shall send forth his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend* (Matthew 13:41-43).$n$,$n$Matthew 13:41 — *The Son of Adam shall send forth his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend, and them which do iniquity;* *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27) Matthew has the Son of Adam send his angels too, but on another errand: they *gather out of his kingdom all things that offend*. Mark shows what the angels do for the elect; Matthew shows that the kingdom is cleared of offenders before the elect are brought in.$n$),
(5289,13,27,$n$And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.$n$,'ezekiel',37,21,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | *I will take the children of Yashar'el (Israel) from among the heathen* (Yechezk'el/Ezekiel 37:21).$n$,$n$Ezekiel 37:21 — *And say unto them, Thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I will take the children of Yashar'el (Israel) from among the heathen, whither they be gone, and will gather them on every side, and bring them into their own land:* *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27) Ezekiel says who is being gathered and from where: *the children of Yashar'el (Israel)*, taken *from among the heathen, whither they be gone*. The elect Mark has coming from the four winds are these scattered children, and Ezekiel says where they are brought, *their own land*.$n$),
(5290,13,27,$n$And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.$n$,'deuteronomy',30,4,$n$thread:wheat-and-tares-the-post-harvest-sifting-architecture | *If any of thine be driven out unto the outmost parts of heaven, from thence will Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) gather thee* (Devarim/Deuteronomy 30:4).$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 30:4 — *If any of thine be driven out unto the outmost parts of heaven, from thence will Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) gather thee, and from thence will he fetch thee:* *And then shall he send his angels, and shall gather together his elect from the four winds, from the uttermost part of the earth to the uttermost part of heaven.* (Mark 13:27) Moses promised that wherever any of the scattered had been driven, even to the outmost parts of heaven, Yahuah (the LORD) himself would gather and fetch them from there. Mark gives the same reach in the mouth of Yahusha (Jesus): the elect come from *the uttermost part of the earth* all the way to *the uttermost part of heaven*.$n$),
(5302,14,3,$n$And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.$n$,'1-samuel',16,13,$n$thread:bridegroom-messianic-identification | *The anointing at Bethany — the woman pouring spikenard on the King's head, anointing-substance the bridegroom-Messiah carries.* The king-anointing pattern from 1 Sh'mu'el (Samuel) 16:13.$n$,$n$1 Samuel 16:13 — *Then Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren: and the Spirit of Yahuah (LORD) came upon David from that day forward. So Samuel rose up, and went to Ramah.* *And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.* (Mark 14:3) Oil poured on a head is how Samuel set David apart: he *anointed him in the midst of his brethren*, and the Spirit of Yahuah (the LORD) came on David from that day. The woman at Bethany pours the ointment *on his head*, and Yahusha (Jesus) says *she is come aforehand to anoint my body to the burying* (Mark 14:8). The anointing of a king and the anointing for the grave are one act.$n$),
(5303,14,3,$n$And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.$n$,'matthew',26,6,$n$thread:bridegroom-messianic-identification | The Matthean parallel of the Bethany anointing.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:6 — *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* *And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.* (Mark 14:3) Both gospels set this in Bethany at the house of Simon the leper. Mark tells more of what the woman did: she *brake the box* of spikenard and poured it on his head, so the whole of it went on him. Matthew gives the same scene without the breaking of the box.$n$),
(5304,14,3,$n$And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.$n$,'john',12,1,$n$thread:bridegroom-messianic-identification | The Johannine parallel — Mary of Bethany anoints the King's feet.$n$,$n$John 12:1 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) six days before the passover came to Bethany, where Lazarus was which had been dead, whom he raised from the dead.* *And being in Bethany in the house of Simon the leper, as he sat at meat, there came a woman having an alabaster box of ointment of spikenard very precious; and she brake the box, and poured it on his head.* (Mark 14:3) John dates the supper *six days before the passover* and places it in Bethany, where Lazarus was. He also names the woman: *Then took Mary a pound of ointment of spikenard, very costly, and anointed the feet of Yahusha (Jesus)* (John 12:3). Mark leaves her unnamed and has the ointment on his head; John gives her name and puts it on his feet.$n$),
(5308,14,12,$n$And the first day of unleavened bread, when they killed the passover, his disciples said unto him, Where will you that we go and prepare that you may eat the passover?$n$,'exodus',12,1,$n$thread:pesach-lamb-fulfillment-substance-the-king-as-the-substantial-pesach-lamb | *The preparation of the Passover — the King ordering the meal he is about to fulfill as its substance* (Shemot/Exodus 12:1-14).$n$,$n$Exodus 12:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt, saying,* *And the first day of unleavened bread, when they killed the passover, his disciples said unto him, Where will you that we go and prepare that you may eat the passover?* (Mark 14:12) The feast the disciples ask about begins in Egypt, with Yahuah (the LORD) speaking to Moses and Aaron, and it is still Moses' feast when they ask where to *prepare that thou mayest eat the passover*. Yahusha (Jesus) does not set it aside; he keeps it as it was given. The lamb the feast turns on, *a lamb for an house* (Exodus 12:3), is the lamb he is about to become.$n$),
(5313,14,25,$n$Verily I say unto you, I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God).$n$,'revelation',19,9,$n$thread:appointed-reign-on-the-earth-and-the-priestly-remnant | *I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God).* The marriage-supper of the Lamb (Revelation 19:9).$n$,$n$Revelation 19:9 — *And he saith unto me, Write, Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb. And he saith unto me, These are the true sayings of Elohim (God).* *Verily I say unto you, I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Mark 14:25) Yahusha (Jesus) says he will not drink the fruit of the vine again until he drinks it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God). Revelation names a table that fits that day: *the marriage supper of the Lamb*, with a blessing on those *called* to it. The cup at the Passover table looks ahead to that supper.$n$),
(5314,14,25,$n$Verily I say unto you, I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God).$n$,'isaiah',25,6,$n$thread:appointed-reign-on-the-earth-and-the-priestly-remnant | *A feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees* (Yeshayahu/Isaiah 25:6-8).$n$,$n$Isaiah 25:6 — *And in this mountain shall Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) make unto all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.* *Verily I say unto you, I will drink no more of the fruit of the vine, until that day that I drink it new in the kingdom of Elohim (God).* (Mark 14:25) Isaiah has Yahuah (the LORD) preparing a feast *in this mountain*, with fat things full of marrow and *wines on the lees well refined*. When Yahusha (Jesus) says he will drink the fruit of the vine new, this is the table the prophet had already described, a feast of wine of the best kind.$n$),
(5327,14,62,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.$n$,'daniel',7,13,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | *The Son of Adam coming in the clouds of heaven* — direct-attribution cloud-rider language naming the Daniyel (Daniel) 7:13 figure as himself.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *I saw in the night visions, and, behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days, and they brought him near before him.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.* (Mark 14:62) Daniel saw *one like the Son of Adam* come *with the clouds of heaven* to the Ancient of days, and the verse after says *there was given him dominion, and glory, and a kingdom* (Daniel 7:14). Yahusha (Jesus) answers the high priest with that vision: the Son of Adam they will see *coming in the clouds of heaven* is the one Daniel saw.$n$),
(5328,14,62,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.$n$,'matthew',26,64,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | The Matthean parallel of the same Sanhedrin-trial cloud-rider self-identification.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:64 — *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto him, Thou hast said: nevertheless I say unto you, Hereafter shall ye see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.* (Mark 14:62) Matthew has Yahusha (Jesus) answer *Thou hast said* and then add *Hereafter shall ye see*, where Mark has him say outright *I am*. The words about the Son of Adam at the right hand and in the clouds are the same in both. What Mark adds is the flat, unhedged answer to the high priest's question.$n$),
(5329,14,62,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.$n$,'revelation',1,7,$n$thread:like-the-son-of-adam-coming-with-the-clouds | Revelation 1:7's *behold, he cometh with clouds*.$n$,$n$Revelation 1:7 — *Behold, he cometh with clouds; and every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him. Even so, Amen.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) said, I am: and you shall see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.* (Mark 14:62) John writes the same coming from the other end: *he cometh with clouds*, and *every eye shall see him*, even *they also which pierced him*. Yahusha (Jesus) told the council *ye shall see*; Revelation says who all will see him, and that *all kindreds of the earth shall wail* because of him.$n$),
(5330,14,61,$n$But he held his peace, and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him, and said unto him, Are you the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the Blessed?$n$,'john',10,36,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | *Art thou the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the Blessed?* The Markan title pairing sits beside the Johannine *I said, I am the Son of Elohim (God)* (Yochanan/John 10:36).$n$,$n$John 10:36 — *Say ye of him, whom the Father hath sanctified, and sent into the world, Thou blasphemest; because I said, I am the Son of Elohim (God)?* *But he held his peace, and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him, and said unto him, Are you the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the Blessed?* (Mark 14:61) The high priest asks whether he is *the Son of the Blessed*, and John shows Yahusha (Jesus) answering the same kind of charge at the feast. He speaks of the one *whom the Father hath sanctified, and sent into the world*, whom they call a blasphemer for saying *I am the Son of Elohim (God)*. The Son they question has a Father who sent him, and they heard the claim well enough to charge him for it.$n$),
(5331,14,61,$n$But he held his peace, and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him, and said unto him, Are you the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the Blessed?$n$,'hebrews',1,1,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | Hebrews 1:1-3's *brightness of his glory, and the express image of his person* — the Formed in the flesh, drawn from the Formless.$n$,$n$Hebrews 1:1 — *Elohim (God), who at sundry times and in divers manners spake in time past unto the fathers by the prophets,* *But he held his peace, and answered nothing. Again the high priest asked him, and said unto him, Are you the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the Blessed?* (Mark 14:61) The high priest asks about *the Son of the Blessed*, and Hebrews opens with the answer in order: Elohim (God) *spake in time past unto the fathers by the prophets*, and in these last days by a Son, *the express image of his person* (Hebrews 1:3). The Father is not seen; the Son is, and he stood before the council.$n$),
(5355,15,39,$n$And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).$n$,'matthew',27,54,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | *Truly this man was the Son of Elohim.* The centurion's confession — the Father drew this one; the centurion heard, even at the cross. The Matthean parallel.$n$,$n$Matthew 27:54 — *Now when the centurion, and they that were with him, watching Yahusha (Jesus), saw the earthquake, and those things that were done, they feared greatly, saying, Truly this was the Son of Elohim (God).* *And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).* (Mark 15:39) Mark gives the centurion one man's death to look at: he saw how Yahusha (Jesus) cried out and gave up the ghost, and said what he said. Matthew gives the same soldier and the men with him the earthquake and all that was done, and they feared greatly. Two tellings, one confession from the man set over the cross: this was the Son of Elohim (God).$n$),
(5356,15,39,$n$And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).$n$,'luke',23,47,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | The Lukan parallel of the centurion's confession.$n$,$n$Luke 23:47 — *Now when the centurion saw what was done, he glorified Elohim (God), saying, Certainly this was a righteous man.* *And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).* (Mark 15:39) Luke keeps the centurion but hears a different sentence from him: he glorified Elohim (God) and called the dead man a righteous man. Mark heard *the Son of Elohim (God)*. The two are not at odds. The righteous one of Isaiah's servant song and the Son are one man on one cross, and the soldier who watched him die says both things across the two gospels.$n$),
(5357,15,39,$n$And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).$n$,'john',19,35,$n$thread:nature-of-god-formed-in-flesh | Yochanan (John) 19:35-37's witness to the substantial cross-event.$n$,$n$John 19:35 — *And he that saw it bare record, and his record is true: and he knoweth that he saith true, that ye might believe.* *And when the centurion, which stood over against him, saw that he so cried out, and gave up the ghost, he said, Truly this man was the Son of Elohim (God).* (Mark 15:39) Mark's witness at the cross is a Roman centurion; John's is the disciple who stood there and bare record, and who says plainly why he wrote it down: *that ye might believe*. A soldier and a disciple, both there, both telling what they saw of that death. John's record of the pierced side and the blood and water sits right after Mark's soldier.$n$);
INSERT INTO _s496_m1_t VALUES
('mark-9-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-and-be-killed-isaiah-53-zechariah-13',$n$The Son of Adam must suffer and be killed — despised, taken from judgment, the shepherd smitten$n$,$n$It ain't new. Coming down from the mountain with the glory still on them, Yahusha (Jesus) tells the three to hold their tongues until the rising.
INSERT INTO _s496_m1_m VALUES
('mark-1-prepare-ye-the-way-of-yahuah-the-forerunner-malachi-3-isaiah-40',4990,1001,$n$Romans 1:1 — *Paul, a servant of Yahusha HaMashiach (Jesus Christ), called to be an apostle, separated unto the gospel of Elohim (God),* Paul says he was separated unto the gospel of Elohim (God), the one his next lines say the prophets had promised beforehand.$n$),
('mark-1-prepare-ye-the-way-of-yahuah-the-forerunner-malachi-3-isaiah-40',4993,1003,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* Yahuah (the LORD) promises to send Elijah the prophet before his great and dreadful day, the forerunner whose voice Mark hears in the wilderness.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5002,1022,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* Yahusha (Jesus) names what the scribes' handed-down tradition does to the commandment, which is why his own teaching sounded so different from theirs.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',5003,1038,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* Matthew gives the same errand in Yahusha's (Jesus's) own words to his disciples: he is sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('scattered-seed-gathering',5004,1038,$n$Ezekiel 34:11 — *For thus saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD); Behold, I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out.* Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) says he himself will search for his sheep and seek them out, the errand Yahusha (Jesus) names at the next towns.$n$),
('mark-1-the-leper-cleansed-sent-to-keep-what-moses-commanded-leviticus-13-14',5005,1040,$n$Leviticus 14:2 — *This shall be the law of the leper in the day of his cleansing: He shall be brought unto the priest:* Moses' law of the leper brings the cleansed man to the priest, the very order Yahusha (Jesus) sends him to keep.$n$),
('mark-2-i-came-not-to-call-the-righteous-but-sinners-hosea-6-ezekiel-34-sirach-18',5010,2014,$n$Ezekiel 34:16 — *I will seek that which was lost, and bring again that which was driven away.* Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) promises to seek the lost and bring back the driven away, which is what happens when Yahusha (Jesus) calls Levi from his seat.$n$),
('mark-2-i-came-not-to-call-the-righteous-but-sinners-hosea-6-ezekiel-34-sirach-18',5011,2017,$n$Ezekiel 34:4 — *neither have ye healed that which was sick, neither have ye bound up that which was broken.* Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) charges the shepherds with leaving the sick unhealed and the broken unbound, the work Yahusha (Jesus) takes up among sinners.$n$),
('mark-2-new-wine-new-garment-the-bridegroom-and-the-renewed-covenant-jeremiah-31-isaiah-54',5012,2019,$n$Hosea 2:19 — *I will betroth thee unto me for ever.* Yahuah (the LORD) promises to betroth the house he put away to himself for ever, the wedding Yahusha (Jesus) steps into as the bridegroom.$n$),
('mark-2-the-sabbath-was-made-for-man-the-son-of-adam-lord-of-the-sabbath-genesis-2-exodus-20-jubilees-2',5016,2023,$n$Leviticus 19:9 — *thou shalt not wholly reap the corners of thy field.* Leviticus leaves the corners and gleanings of the field for the poor and the stranger, so the Torah itself makes room for the hungry in a field.$n$),
('mark-2-the-sabbath-was-made-for-man-the-son-of-adam-lord-of-the-sabbath-genesis-2-exodus-20-jubilees-2',5017,2023,$n$Deuteronomy 23:25 — *then thou mayest pluck the ears with thine hand.* Moses allows a hungry man to pluck ears by hand in his neighbour's standing corn, which is exactly what the disciples were doing.$n$),
('mark-2-the-sabbath-was-made-for-man-the-son-of-adam-lord-of-the-sabbath-genesis-2-exodus-20-jubilees-2',5018,2025,$n$1 Samuel 21:1 — *Then came David to Nob to Ahimelech the priest.* David comes hungry to the priest at Nob, the story Yahusha (Jesus) names when the disciples are charged for plucking grain on the sabbath.$n$),
('mark-3-is-it-lawful-to-do-good-on-the-sabbath-the-withered-hand-deuteronomy-22-isaiah-58',5022,3001,$n$Exodus 20:8 — *Remember the sabbath day, to keep it holy.* The fourth commandment tells Yashar'el (Israel) to remember the sabbath day and keep it holy, the commandment Yahusha (Jesus) keeps while he heals.$n$),
('mark-3-is-it-lawful-to-do-good-on-the-sabbath-the-withered-hand-deuteronomy-22-isaiah-58',5023,3001,$n$Deuteronomy 5:12 — *Keep the sabbath day to sanctify it, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee.* Moses gives the sabbath commandment a second time in Deuteronomy, the day Yahusha (Jesus) keeps in the synagogue before he heals.$n$),
('mark-3-is-it-lawful-to-do-good-on-the-sabbath-the-withered-hand-deuteronomy-22-isaiah-58',5024,3006,$n$John 11:47 — *Then gathered the chief priests and the Pharisees a council, and said, What do we? for this man doeth many miracles.* After Lazarus is raised, the chief priests and Pharisees gather in council over the many miracles, as the Pharisees plotted over the withered hand.$n$),
('mark-3-he-ordained-twelve-the-tribes-re-gathered-exodus-24-genesis-49',5027,3014,$n$Ezekiel 37:15 — *The word of Yahuah (LORD) came again unto me, saying,* This opens Ezekiel's word of the two sticks, Yahudah (Judah) and the house of Yashar'el (Israel), which Yahuah (the LORD) will join into one.$n$),
('mark-3-he-ordained-twelve-the-tribes-re-gathered-exodus-24-genesis-49',5028,3014,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written thereon.* The city's wall has twelve gates with the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel) written on them.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5029,3022,$n$Mark 7:13 — *Making the word of Elohim (God) of none effect through your tradition, which ye have delivered: and many such like things do ye.* Yahusha (Jesus) says the tradition of the elders makes the word of Elohim (God) of none effect, the habit on show when scribes call his work Beelzebub's.$n$),
('mark-3-blasphemy-against-the-ruach-hakodesh-the-presumptuous-sin-isaiah-49-numbers-15',5030,3028,$n$1 John 5:16 — *There is a sin unto death: I do not say that he shall pray for it.* John separates a sin unto death from the sins for which a brother may ask and be given life, and does not say to pray for that one.$n$),
('mark-3-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-elohim-the-same-is-my-brother-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',5031,3033,$n$Hosea 1:10 — *there it shall be said unto them, Ye are the sons of the living Elohim (God).* Where the house of Yashar'el (Israel) was told it was not his people, it is told it is the sons of the living Elohim (God).$n$),
('mark-3-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-elohim-the-same-is-my-brother-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',5032,3035,$n$Matthew 7:21 — *but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.* Matthew has him say that not everyone who calls him Adonai (Lord) enters the kingdom, but the one who does the will of his Father.$n$),
('my-sheep-hear-my-voice-hearing-reveals-not-creates',5034,4003,$n$John 10:26 — *But ye believe not, because ye are not of my sheep, as I said unto you.* Yahusha (Jesus) says they do not believe because they are not of his sheep, and the next verse says his sheep hear his voice.$n$),
('matthew-13-the-sower-why-parables-seeing-they-see-not-isaiah-6-isaiah-55',5036,4014,$n$Matthew 13:18 — *Hear ye therefore the parable of the sower.* Matthew opens the explanation of the sower by telling the disciples to hear, right after calling their eyes and ears blessed.$n$),
('mark-4-the-seed-sown-and-the-evil-seed-in-the-heart-of-adam-in-2-esdras',5037,4014,$n$Ezekiel 20:37 — *And I will cause you to pass under the rod, and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant:* Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) causes the gathered house to pass under the rod before he brings it into the bond of the covenant.$n$),
('mark-4-the-seed-sown-and-the-evil-seed-in-the-heart-of-adam-in-2-esdras',5038,4017,$n$Hebrews 6:4 — *those who were once enlightened, and have tasted of the heavenly gift.* Hebrews speaks of those once enlightened who tasted the heavenly gift and shared in the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit), and warns what follows if they fall away.$n$),
('mark-4-the-seed-sown-and-the-evil-seed-in-the-heart-of-adam-in-2-esdras',5039,4019,$n$1 Timothy 6:9 — *But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition.* Paul warns Timothy that those who will be rich fall into a snare and into lusts that drown men in destruction, as the thorns choke the word.$n$),
('mark-4-the-candle-not-under-a-bushel-psalm-119-and-ecclesiastes-12',5040,4022,$n$Ecclesiastes 12:14 — *For Elohim (God) shall bring every work into judgment, with every secret thing, whether it be good, or whether it be evil.* The Preacher ends by saying Elohim (God) will bring every work into judgment, with every secret thing, good or evil.$n$),
('mark-4-the-candle-not-under-a-bushel-psalm-119-and-ecclesiastes-12',5042,4025,$n$Revelation 13:8 — *whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world.* Those who worship the beast are the ones whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb from the foundation of the world.$n$),
('mark-4-peace-be-still-the-creators-authority-over-the-sea-psalm-107-and-job-38',5045,4035,$n$Psalm 89:9 — *Thou rulest the raging of the sea: when the waves thereof arise, thou stillest them.* The psalm says Yahuah (the LORD) rules the raging sea and stills the waves when they rise, the work Yahusha (Jesus) does from the boat.$n$),
('gadarene-demoniac-legion-and-the-eastern-territory-of-the-scattered',5049,5007,$n$James 2:19 — *Thou believest that there is one Elohim (God); thou doest well: the devils also believe, and tremble.* James says even the devils believe in one Elohim (God) and tremble, which is what the unclean spirit does when it meets Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
('gadarene-demoniac-legion-and-the-eastern-territory-of-the-scattered',5050,5007,$n$Genesis 14:18 — *And Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) king of Salem brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the El Elyon (most high God).* Melek Tsadiq (Melchizedek) brings bread and wine to Abram as priest of the El Elyon (most high God), the same title the spirit uses.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5051,5023,$n$Matthew 9:18 — *My daughter is even now dead: but come and lay thy hand upon her, and she shall live.* Matthew gives the ruler's plea after the child has already died, asking Yahusha (Jesus) to lay his hand on her so that she shall live.$n$),
('talitha-cumi-and-the-twelve-year-resurrection-of-the-daughter-of-the-house',5052,5023,$n$Luke 8:41 — *there came a man named Jairus, and he was a ruler of the synagogue.* Luke names Jairus, says he was a ruler of the synagogue, and has him fall at Yahusha's (Jesus's) feet asking him into his house.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5053,5025,$n$Leviticus 15:25 — *if a woman have an issue of her blood many days out of the time of her separation.* Leviticus says the woman whose flow runs on past its time is unclean all the days of the issue, which is the twelve years Mark counts.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5054,5025,$n$Numbers 15:38 — *Speak unto the children of Yashar'el (Israel), and bid them that they make them fringes in the borders of their garments throughout their generations, and that they put upon the fringe of the borders a ribband of blue:* Numbers commands fringes with a blue cord on the borders of the garments so that Yashar'el (Israel) would remember all the commandments and do them.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5055,5025,$n$Jeremiah 23:6 — *In his days Yahudah (Judah) shall be saved, and Yashar'el (Israel) shall dwell safely: and this is his name whereby he shall be called, Yahuah Tsidkenu (THE LORD OUR RIGHTEOUSNESS).* Jeremiah says both houses are saved and dwell safely in the days of the righteous Branch, who is named as our righteousness.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5056,5034,$n$Habakkuk 2:4 — *Behold, his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith.* Habakkuk sets the proud soul beside the just man who lives by his faith, the trust the woman showed in reaching for him.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5057,5034,$n$Psalm 27:13 — *I had fainted, unless I had believed to see the goodness of Yahuah (LORD) in the land of the living.* The psalmist would have fainted had he not believed he would see the goodness of Yahuah (the LORD) in the land of the living.$n$),
('matthew-9-the-hem-of-his-garment-the-fringe-healing-in-his-wings-numbers-15-malachi-4',5058,5034,$n$Isaiah 7:9 — *If ye will not believe, surely ye shall not be established.* Isaiah says that without believing there is no standing, and the verse that says it names Ephraim, whose head is Samaria.$n$),
('talitha-cumi-and-the-twelve-year-resurrection-of-the-daughter-of-the-house',5059,5036,$n$Psalm 27:13 — *I had fainted, unless I had believed to see the goodness of Yahuah (LORD) in the land of the living.* The psalmist held on to seeing the goodness of Yahuah (the LORD) while he lived, which is what Jairus is told to hold on to.$n$),
('mark-6-a-prophet-not-without-honour-rejected-by-his-own-1-samuel-8-jeremiah-11-isaiah-53',5063,6004,$n$1 Kings 19:10 — *the children of Yashar'el (Israel) have forsaken thy covenant, thrown down thine altars, and slain thy prophets with the sword.* Elijah tells Yahuah (the LORD) that Yashar'el (Israel) has slain his prophets and now seeks his own life, the long record Yahusha's (Jesus's) saying draws on.$n$),
('matthew-13-the-scribe-brings-things-new-and-old-a-prophet-without-honour-jeremiah-6',5064,6004,$n$Matthew 13:57 — *A prophet is not without honour, save in his own country, and in his own house.* Matthew gives the same saying right after the people of Nazareth are offended in him, naming his country and his house but not his kin.$n$),
('nazareth-scroll-the-acceptable-year-stopped-mid-verse-and-the-individual-mercy-contrast-cases',5065,6004,$n$Luke 4:24 — *And he said, Verily I say unto you, No prophet is accepted in his own country.* Luke says no prophet is accepted in his own country and goes straight on to Elijah and Elisha, so the saying becomes an argument about whom the prophet was sent to.$n$),
('matthew-10-the-twelve-called-and-the-twelve-tribes',5067,6007,$n$Matthew 10:1 — *he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease.* Matthew counts the twelve he called and adds that their power reached to every sickness and every disease, not only to unclean spirits.$n$),
('matthew-10-the-twelve-called-and-the-twelve-tribes',5068,6007,$n$Revelation 21:12 — *names written thereon, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Yashar'el (Israel).* Revelation gives the city twelve gates with an angel at each and the names of the twelve tribes of Yashar'el (Israel) written on them.$n$),
('matthew-10-shake-off-the-dust-and-sodom',5069,6011,$n$Matthew 10:14 — *And whosoever shall not receive you, nor hear your words, when ye depart out of that house or city, shake off the dust of your feet.* Matthew has the twelve shake the dust from their feet when a house or city will not receive them or hear their words.$n$),
('matthew-10-shake-off-the-dust-and-sodom',5070,6011,$n$Acts 13:51 — *But they shook off the dust of their feet against them, and came unto Iconium.* Paul and Barnabas shake the dust from their feet against the city that drove them out and move on to Iconium.$n$),
('my-sheep-hear-my-voice-hearing-reveals-not-creates',5071,6011,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* Yahusha (Jesus) says his sheep hear his voice, which is how the ones he already knows are shown when the word is sent out.$n$),
('matthew-7-give-not-that-which-is-holy-unto-the-dogs-pearls-before-swine-proverbs-9-23',5072,6011,$n$Matthew 7:6 — *neither cast ye your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet.* Matthew warns against giving what is holy to dogs or casting pearls before swine, who trample them and turn on the giver.$n$),
('mark-6-john-beheaded-the-forerunner-slain-leviticus-18-1-kings-19-sirach-9',5075,6014,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* The word of Yahuah (the LORD) comes to Elijah to send him to Ahab, in the vineyard of Naboth whom Ahab had killed for it.$n$),
('mark-9-elias-must-first-come-and-restore-all-things-restore-the-tribes-of-jacob-malachi-4-sirach-48',5076,6014,$n$Mark 9:13 — *But I say unto you, That Elijah is indeed come, and they have done unto him whatsoever they listed, as it is written of him.* Yahusha (Jesus) says Elijah has come and was treated as men pleased, which names John and what was done to him.$n$),
('mark-6-john-beheaded-the-forerunner-slain-leviticus-18-1-kings-19-sirach-9',5077,6018,$n$1 Kings 21:17 — *And the word of Yahuah (LORD) came to Elijah the Tishbite, saying,* The word of Yahuah (the LORD) sends Elijah to Ahab with a reproof, and behind Ahab stands Jezebel, who arranged the taking of the vineyard.$n$),
('mark-6-john-beheaded-the-forerunner-slain-leviticus-18-1-kings-19-sirach-9',5078,6018,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper.* Zechariah speaks the commandments of Yahuah (the LORD) to the people and the king, and is stoned for it.$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5079,6030,$n$Numbers 27:17 — *that the congregation of Yahuah (LORD) be not as sheep which have no shepherd.* Moses asks Yahuah (the LORD) for a leader who will go out and come in before the people so they are not like sheep without a shepherd.$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5080,6030,$n$Ezekiel 34:5 — *And they were scattered, because there is no shepherd: and they became meat to all the beasts of the field, when they were scattered.* Ezekiel says the flock was scattered and became prey because there was no shepherd, and the failed shepherds are the cause.$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5081,6030,$n$1 Kings 22:17 — *I saw all Yashar'el (Israel) scattered upon the hills, as sheep that have not a shepherd.* Micaiah sees all Yashar'el (Israel) scattered like sheep with no shepherd, and the word of Yahuah (the LORD) is that they go home in peace.$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5087,6037,$n$2 Kings 4:42 — *brought the man of Elohim (God) bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley.* Elisha is brought twenty loaves of barley and tells his servant to give them to the people to eat, with some left over.$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5088,6037,$n$Ezekiel 34:14 — *I will feed them in a good pasture, and upon the high mountains of Yashar'el (Israel) shall their fold be.* Yahuah (the LORD) promises to feed the gathered flock in a good pasture on the mountains of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('mark-6-feeding-the-five-thousand-sheep-without-a-shepherd-numbers-27-ezekiel-34-2-kings-4',5089,6037,$n$Ezekiel 34:23 — *And I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them, even my servant David; he shall feed them, and he shall be their shepherd.* Ezekiel says Yahuah (the LORD) will set up one shepherd, his servant David, who will feed the flock and be their shepherd.$n$),
('mark-6-walking-upon-the-sea-it-is-i-the-i-am-job-9-exodus-3-psalm-77',5092,6048,$n$Exodus 33:19 — *I will make all my goodness pass before thee, and I will proclaim the name of Yahuah (LORD) before thee.* Yahuah (the LORD) tells Moses he will make his goodness pass before him and proclaim his name, which is the passing Mark's words recall.$n$),
('mark-6-walking-upon-the-sea-it-is-i-the-i-am-job-9-exodus-3-psalm-77',5093,6048,$n$1 Kings 19:11 — *And, behold, Yahuah (LORD) passed by, and a great and strong wind rent the mountains, and brake in pieces the rocks before Yahuah (LORD).* Yahuah (the LORD) passes by Elijah on the mountain with a great wind, an earthquake and a fire before the still small voice.$n$),
('mark-6-walking-upon-the-sea-it-is-i-the-i-am-job-9-exodus-3-psalm-77',5098,6052,$n$Isaiah 6:9 — *And he said, Go, and tell this people, Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not.* Isaiah is told to say that the people hear and do not understand and see and do not perceive.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5099,7003,$n$Leviticus 22:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses, saying,* The word Yahuah (the LORD) spoke to Moses about the holy things was for Aaron and his sons, not for every man's table.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5100,7006,$n$Isaiah 29:13 — *their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men.* Isaiah says the people honoured Yahuah (the LORD) with mouth and lips while their fear of him had become something men taught them.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5101,7008,$n$Deuteronomy 4:2 — *Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it, that ye may keep the commandments of Yahuah Elohaychem (the LORD your God) which I command you.* Moses forbids adding to or taking from the commandments, the rule the washing of pots and cups breaks.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5102,7008,$n$Deuteronomy 12:32 — *What thing soever I command you, observe to do it: thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it.* Moses repeats that nothing is to be added to or taken from what Yahuah (the LORD) commanded.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5103,7009,$n$Exodus 20:12 — *Honour thy father and thy mother: that thy days may be long upon the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* Honour thy father and thy mother is the commandment the Corban saying set aside.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5104,7009,$n$Deuteronomy 5:16 — *Honour thy father and thy mother, as Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) hath commanded thee; that thy days may be prolonged, and that it may go well with thee, in the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee.* Moses repeats honour of father and mother as the commandment Yahuah (the LORD) gave, with a promise that it may go well.$n$),
('mark-7-the-corban-tradition-voids-the-fifth-commandment-in-sirach',5105,7009,$n$1 John 2:3 — *And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments.* John makes keeping his commandments the way we know we know him, the test the Pharisees' tradition fails.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5108,7014,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* Jeremiah says the heart is deceitful above all things, which is where Yahusha (Jesus) says what defiles a man comes from.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5109,7014,$n$Deuteronomy 30:6 — *will circumcise thine heart, and the heart of thy seed.* Moses promises that Yahuah (the LORD) himself will circumcise the heart, the cure the Torah already holds for a defiled heart.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5110,7019,$n$Leviticus 11:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and to Aaron, saying unto them,* Leviticus 11 is where Yahuah (the LORD) told Moses and Aaron which beasts Yashar'el (Israel) may eat, a question Mark's sentence about the belly does not answer.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5111,7019,$n$Acts 10:14 — *But Peter said, Not so, Yahuah (Lord); for I have never eaten any thing that is common or unclean.* Peter, years after the resurrection, still says he has never eaten anything common or unclean, so the list Moses gave still stood for the apostles.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5112,7021,$n$Exodus 20:13 — *Thou shalt not kill.* Thou shalt not kill is the commandment broken by the murders Yahusha (Jesus) lists among what comes out of the heart.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5113,7021,$n$Jeremiah 17:9 — *The heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked: who can know it?* Jeremiah calls the heart desperately wicked, the heart that produces the list Yahusha (Jesus) reads out in Mark.$n$),
('mark-7-defilement-from-within-the-heart-the-source-of-evil-in-sirach',5114,7021,$n$Ezekiel 36:26 — *A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you.* Yahuah (the LORD) promises a new heart and a new spirit within, the answer to the heart that brings out the list Yahusha (Jesus) read.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',5115,7024,$n$Matthew 15:21 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) went thence, and departed into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon.* Matthew sends him to the coasts of Tyre and Sidon straight from the hand-washing talk, and a woman of Canaan comes out of the same coasts.$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',5116,7024,$n$Matthew 15:24 — *But he answered and said, I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).* Matthew gives the answer Yahusha (Jesus) made to his disciples about her: he was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('matthew-15-the-canaanite-woman-the-lost-sheep-first-the-stranger-grafted-isaiah-56-psalm-22-1-kings-17',5117,7028,$n$Matthew 15:27 — *And she said, Truth, Adonai (Lord): yet the dogs eat of the crumbs which fall from their masters’ table.* Matthew's woman says Truth and puts the dogs at their masters' table, accepting the order he gave her and asking only for the crumbs.$n$),
('matthew-12-the-sign-of-the-prophet-jonah-greater-than-solomon-jonah-1-jonah-3-1-kings-10',5121,8011,$n$Matthew 12:38 — *Then certain of the scribes and of the Pharisees answered, saying, Master, we would see a sign from thee.* Matthew's scribes and Pharisees ask politely, calling him Master, for a sign, where Mark says they came tempting him.$n$),
('matthew-16-no-sign-but-the-prophet-jonah-the-signs-of-the-times-jonah-1',5122,8011,$n$Matthew 16:1 — *The Pharisees also with the Sadducees came, and tempting desired him that he would shew them a sign from heaven.* Matthew names the Sadducees beside the Pharisees as the ones who came tempting him and asked for a sign from heaven.$n$),
('matthew-16-no-sign-but-the-prophet-jonah-the-signs-of-the-times-jonah-1',5123,8011,$n$Jonah 1:17 — *And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.* Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the fish, the one sign the sign-seekers are left with.$n$),
('mark-8-leaven-of-the-pharisees-and-of-herod-as-conjoined-political-religious-corruption',5124,8015,$n$Exodus 12:15 — *the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses.* Moses commands that leaven be put out of the houses at Passover, the picture Yahusha (Jesus) borrows for teaching that spreads.$n$),
('mark-8-leaven-of-the-pharisees-and-of-herod-as-conjoined-political-religious-corruption',5127,8017,$n$Isaiah 6:9 — *And he said, Go, and tell this people, Hear ye indeed, but understand not; and see ye indeed, but perceive not.* Isaiah is told to say to the people, hear indeed but understand not, the words Yahusha (Jesus) turns on the twelve.$n$),
('mark-8-leaven-of-the-pharisees-and-of-herod-as-conjoined-political-religious-corruption',5128,8017,$n$Jeremiah 5:21 — *Hear now this, O foolish people, and without understanding; which have eyes, and see not; which have ears, and hear not:* Jeremiah calls a foolish people without understanding who have eyes and see not and ears and hear not, the words Yahusha (Jesus) puts to the twelve.$n$),
('mark-8-leaven-of-the-pharisees-and-of-herod-as-conjoined-political-religious-corruption',5129,8017,$n$Ezekiel 12:2 — *which have eyes to see, and see not; they have ears to hear, and hear not.* Ezekiel is told the rebellious house has eyes and ears and does not use them, the complaint Yahusha (Jesus) brings to his own disciples.$n$),
('mark-8-the-blind-man-of-bethsaida-healed-in-stages-isaiah-35-isaiah-42',5131,8022,$n$Isaiah 42:7 — *To open the blind eyes, to bring out the prisoners from the prison, and them that sit in darkness out of the prison house.* Isaiah names opening the blind eyes and bringing prisoners out of darkness as the work of the one sent, and Bethsaida shows it done.$n$),
('matthew-16-thou-art-the-messiah-the-rock-and-the-keys-psalm-2-isaiah-22-isaiah-28',5132,8027,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* Matthew's question has Yahusha (Jesus) calling himself the Son of Adam as he asks whom men say he is.$n$),
('luke-9-the-messiah-of-elohim-confessed-and-the-suffering-foretold-psalm-2-isaiah-53',5133,8027,$n$Luke 9:18 — *And it came to pass, as he was alone praying, his disciples were with him: and he asked them, saying, Whom say the people that I am?* Luke sets the question while Yahusha (Jesus) is alone praying with his disciples near, and asks what the people say of him.$n$),
('mark-8-thou-art-the-messiah-peters-confession-psalm-2-daniel-7-1-enoch-46-48',5134,8027,$n$John 6:69 — *And we believe and are sure that thou art that Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).* John's Peter speaks for the group, we believe and are sure, and names Yahusha (Jesus) the Messiah (Christ), the Son of the living Elohim (God).$n$),
('mark-8-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-then-come-in-glory-isaiah-53-psalm-49-daniel-7-zechariah-14',5136,8031,$n$Psalm 22:6 — *But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people.* Psalm 22 has the sufferer say he is despised of the people and a reproach of men, the shame Yahusha (Jesus) says awaits the Son of Adam.$n$),
('mark-8-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-then-come-in-glory-isaiah-53-psalm-49-daniel-7-zechariah-14',5137,8031,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him.* Zechariah has the house of David and Jerusalem look on the one they pierced and mourn for him as for an only son.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',5138,8031,$n$Matthew 16:21 — *he must go unto Jerusalem, and suffer many things of the elders and chief priests and scribes.* Matthew names Jerusalem as where Yahusha (Jesus) must go to suffer and be killed, and says he would be raised the third day.$n$),
('mark-8-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-then-come-in-glory-isaiah-53-psalm-49-daniel-7-zechariah-14',5142,8033,$n$Mark 7:8 — *ye hold the tradition of men, as the washing of pots and cups.* Earlier Yahusha (Jesus) told the Pharisees they laid aside the commandment of Elohim (God) to hold the tradition of men, the fault he now names in Peter.$n$),
('matthew-16-get-thee-behind-me-satan-he-must-suffer-many-things-isaiah-53',5143,8033,$n$Matthew 16:23 — *thou art an offence unto me: for thou savourest not the things that be of Elohim (God).* Matthew adds that Yahusha (Jesus) told Peter he was an offence unto him, as well as one who savoured the things of men.$n$),
('matthew-10-not-peace-but-a-sword-a-mans-foes-lose-your-life-micah-7-deuteronomy-33',5144,8034,$n$Matthew 10:38 — *And he that taketh not his cross, and followeth after me, is not worthy of me.* Matthew gives it as a measure: the man who does not take his cross and follow Yahusha (Jesus) is not worthy of him.$n$),
('matthew-16-take-up-the-cross-the-son-of-man-shall-come-in-glory-rewarding-psalm-49-daniel-7',5145,8034,$n$Matthew 16:24 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto his disciples, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow me.* Matthew has the same call to deny himself, take up his cross and follow, spoken to his disciples.$n$),
('luke-9-take-up-thy-cross-and-the-glory-of-the-son-of-adam-daniel-7-psalm-49',5146,8034,$n$Luke 9:23 — *And he said to them all, If any man will come after me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily, and follow me.* Luke says Yahusha (Jesus) spoke to them all and that the cross is taken up daily.$n$),
('count-the-cost-tower-builder-king-going-to-war-and-the-disciple-who-forsakes-not-all-he-hath',5147,8034,$n$Luke 14:27 — *And whosoever doth not bear his cross, and come after me, cannot be my disciple.* Luke turns the call around: whoever does not bear his cross and come after Yahusha (Jesus) cannot be his disciple.$n$),
('mark-9-transfiguration-moses-and-elijah-hear-him-the-fathers-voice-deut-18-malachi-4-daniel-7',5160,9001,$n$Daniel 7:14 — *his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which shall not pass away.* Daniel says the dominion and kingdom given to the one he saw are everlasting and will not pass away or be destroyed.$n$),
('mark-9-transfiguration-moses-and-elijah-hear-him-the-fathers-voice-deut-18-malachi-4-daniel-7',5164,9004,$n$2 Kings 2:11 — *Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven.* Elijah did not die; a chariot of fire parted him from Elisha and he went up by a whirlwind into heaven.$n$),
('mark-9-transfiguration-moses-and-elijah-hear-him-the-fathers-voice-deut-18-malachi-4-daniel-7',5165,9004,$n$Deuteronomy 34:5 — *So Moses the servant of Yahuah (LORD) died there in the land of Moab, according to the word of Yahuah (LORD).* Moses died in the land of Moab by the word of Yahuah (the LORD), and now appears beside Elijah with Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
('mark-9-elias-must-first-come-and-restore-all-things-restore-the-tribes-of-jacob-malachi-4-sirach-48',5174,9013,$n$1 Kings 19:2 — *So let the gods do to me, and more also, if I make not thy life as the life of one of them.* Jezebel swears to take Elijah's life by the next day, the threat that the prophet in Elijah's power meets again in John.$n$),
('mark-9-elias-must-first-come-and-restore-all-things-restore-the-tribes-of-jacob-malachi-4-sirach-48',5175,9013,$n$Malachi 4:5 — *Behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of Yahuah (LORD):* Malachi promises Elijah the prophet before the great and dreadful day, the promise Yahusha (Jesus) says John fulfils.$n$),
('mark-9-elias-must-first-come-and-restore-all-things-restore-the-tribes-of-jacob-malachi-4-sirach-48',5176,9013,$n$2 Chronicles 24:20 — *Why transgress ye the commandments of Yahuah (LORD), that ye cannot prosper?* Zechariah, filled by the Spirit of Elohim (God), told the people they had forsaken the commandments of Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('mark-9-elias-must-first-come-and-restore-all-things-restore-the-tribes-of-jacob-malachi-4-sirach-48',5177,9013,$n$Matthew 23:35 — *from the blood of righteous Abel unto the blood of Zechariah son of Barachias.* Matthew counts the righteous blood from Abel to Zechariah, slain between the temple and the altar, as the line John stands in.$n$),
('mark-9-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-and-be-killed-isaiah-53-zechariah-13',5172,9009,$n$Isaiah 53:3 — *He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief.* Isaiah draws the man despised, rejected and acquainted with grief, the road that lies between the mountain and the rising.$n$),
('mark-9-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-and-be-killed-isaiah-53-zechariah-13',5181,9031,$n$Isaiah 53:8 — *he was cut off out of the land of the living: for the transgression of my people was he stricken.* Isaiah says he was taken from prison and judgment and cut off from the living because of the transgression of my people.$n$),
('mark-9-the-son-of-adam-must-suffer-and-be-killed-isaiah-53-zechariah-13',5182,9031,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* Zechariah says the shepherd is smitten and the sheep are scattered, with the hand turned upon the little ones.$n$),
('matthew-12-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-my-father-the-true-family-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',5185,9037,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* Matthew says whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is brother, sister and mother to Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
('mark-9-who-should-be-the-greatest-the-last-of-all-and-the-little-child-proverbs-15-isaiah-57',5186,9037,$n$John 13:20 — *Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that receiveth whomsoever I send receiveth me; and he that receiveth me receiveth him that sent me.* John says whoever receives the one Yahusha (Jesus) sends receives him, and receives the Father who sent him.$n$),
('mark-10-from-the-beginning-male-and-female-divorce-and-the-creation-torah-genesis-1-2-deuteronomy-24-malachi-2',5196,10006,$n$Genesis 2:24 — *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* Genesis says a man leaves his father and mother, cleaves to his wife, and the two become one flesh.$n$),
('mark-10-suffer-the-little-children-of-such-is-the-kingdom-psalm-8-deuteronomy-1-joel-2',5197,10014,$n$Matthew 18:3 — *And said, Verily I say unto you, Except ye be converted, and become as little children, ye shall not enter into the kingdom of heaven.* Matthew says unless the disciples turn and become as little children they will not enter the kingdom of heaven.$n$),
('mark-10-suffer-the-little-children-of-such-is-the-kingdom-psalm-8-deuteronomy-1-joel-2',5198,10014,$n$Psalm 131:1 — *Yahuah (LORD), my heart is not haughty, nor mine eyes lofty: neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me.* The psalmist says his heart is not haughty and he does not reach for things too high for him.$n$),
('matthew-12-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-my-father-the-true-family-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',5203,10028,$n$Matthew 12:50 — *For whosoever shall do the will of my Father which is in heaven, the same is my brother, and sister, and mother.* Matthew says whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is his brother, sister and mother.$n$),
('mark-3-whosoever-shall-do-the-will-of-elohim-the-same-is-my-brother-deuteronomy-33-psalm-22',5204,10028,$n$Mark 3:35 — *For whosoever shall do the will of Elohim (God), the same is my brother, and my sister, and mother.* Mark 3 says whoever does the will of Elohim (God) is his brother, sister and mother, the household named again at the hundredfold.$n$),
('many-be-called-but-few-chosen-in-1-enoch-and-2-esdras',5205,10031,$n$Matthew 22:14 — *For many are called, but few are chosen.* Matthew closes the wedding parable with many called and few chosen, the choosing the Father's own.$n$),
('the-gathering-of-the-twelve-tribes-and-the-thrones-of-judgment-in-jubilees-sirach-wisdom-and-testaments-xii',5206,10031,$n$Matthew 19:30 — *But many that are first shall be last; and the last shall be first.* Matthew closes the answer to Peter with the same saying, first made last and the last first.$n$),
('luke-13-they-shall-come-from-east-and-west-the-gathering-of-the-tribes-in-baruch-and-sirach',5207,10031,$n$Luke 13:30 — *And, behold, there are last which shall be first, and there are first which shall be last.* Luke puts the saying beside the fathers in the kingdom and the ones who come from the four directions to sit down.$n$),
('mark-10-the-cup-and-the-ransom-for-many-the-suffering-servant-isaiah-53-isaiah-51-daniel-7',5208,10032,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb.* Isaiah says he was oppressed and afflicted yet opened not his mouth, brought as a lamb to the slaughter.$n$),
('mark-10-the-cup-and-the-ransom-for-many-the-suffering-servant-isaiah-53-isaiah-51-daniel-7',5209,10032,$n$Psalm 22:6 — *But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised of the people.* The psalm speaks of one despised of the people, a reproach of men, the road Yahusha (Jesus) walks up to Jerusalem.$n$),
('mark-10-blind-bartimaeus-thou-son-of-david-have-mercy-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',5214,10046,$n$Jeremiah 23:5 — *Behold, the days come, saith Yahuah (LORD), that I will raise unto David a righteous Branch, and a King shall reign and prosper, and shall execute judgment and justice in the earth.* Jeremiah promises a righteous Branch raised to David who shall reign, prosper and execute judgment and justice in the earth.$n$),
('mark-10-blind-bartimaeus-thou-son-of-david-have-mercy-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',5215,10046,$n$2 Samuel 7:12 — *I will set up thy seed after thee, which shall proceed out of thy bowels, and I will establish his kingdom.* Samuel records the promise to David of a seed from his own body whose kingdom Yahuah (the LORD) will establish.$n$),
('mark-10-blind-bartimaeus-thou-son-of-david-have-mercy-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',5216,10046,$n$Isaiah 11:1 — *And there shall come forth a rod out of the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots:* Isaiah promises a rod from the stem of Jesse and a Branch growing out of his roots.$n$),
('mark-10-blind-bartimaeus-thou-son-of-david-have-mercy-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',5217,10052,$n$John 10:27 — *My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me:* John says his sheep hear his voice, are known by him, and follow him.$n$),
('mark-10-blind-bartimaeus-thou-son-of-david-have-mercy-isaiah-35-psalm-146-2-samuel-7',5218,10052,$n$Revelation 13:8 — *whose names are not written in the book of life of the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world.* Revelation says the book of life belongs to the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world and names are written in it from then.$n$),
('mark-11-the-barren-fig-tree-and-the-forgiveness-that-frees-prayer-jeremiah-8-hosea-9-sirach-28',5230,11025,$n$Leviticus 19:18 — *Thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people, but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself: I am Yahuah (LORD).* Leviticus forbids vengeance and grudges against the children of thy people and commands love for the neighbour as thyself.$n$),
('matthew-6-forgive-men-their-trespasses-as-we-forgive-sirach-28-mark-11',5231,11025,$n$Matthew 6:14 — *For if ye forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you:* Matthew says if you forgive men their trespasses your heavenly Father will also forgive you.$n$),
('mark-11-by-what-authority-the-sent-one-challenged-as-moses-was-exodus-2-numbers-16-malachi-3',5232,11027,$n$Matthew 16:13 — *When Yahusha (Jesus) came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of Adam am?* Matthew has Yahusha (Jesus) ask what men say of him, the same ground of who he is and by whose authority he acts.$n$),
('matthew-21-by-what-authority',5233,11027,$n$Matthew 21:23 — *the chief priests and the elders of the people came unto him as he was teaching.* Matthew has the chief priests and elders of the people come to him as he teaches in the temple with the same question.$n$),
('mark-12-the-wicked-husbandmen-and-the-one-chosen-vine-in-2-esdras',5236,12001,$n$Romans 11:5 — *Even so then at this present time also there is a remnant according to the election of grace.* Paul says a remnant according to the election of grace stands in his own day, a chosen part out of the house.$n$),
('mark-12-the-wicked-husbandmen-and-the-one-chosen-vine-in-2-esdras',5237,12001,$n$Isaiah 10:21 — *The remnant shall return, even the remnant of Jacob, unto El Gibbor (the mighty God).* Isaiah says the remnant of Jacob shall return to El Gibbor (the mighty God).$n$),
('mark-12-the-elohim-of-the-living-and-the-souls-of-the-righteous-in-wisdom-of-solomon',5244,12026,$n$Exodus 3:6 — *I am the Elohim (God) of thy father, the Elohim (God) of Abraham, the Elohim (God) of Isaac, and the Elohim (God) of Jacob.* At the bush Elohim (God) names himself the Elohim (God) of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, the words Yahusha (Jesus) brings to the Sadducees.$n$),
('mark-12-the-elohim-of-the-living-and-the-souls-of-the-righteous-in-wisdom-of-solomon',5245,12026,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt.* Daniel says many who sleep in the dust shall awake, some to everlasting life and some to shame and contempt.$n$),
('mark-12-the-elohim-of-the-living-and-the-souls-of-the-righteous-in-wisdom-of-solomon',5246,12026,$n$Hebrews 11:13 — *These all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off.* Hebrews says the patriarchs died in faith without receiving the promises, having seen them afar off and embraced them.$n$),
('mark-12-more-than-all-whole-burnt-offerings-the-scribe-says-what-the-prophets-said',5252,12032,$n$Hosea 6:6 — *For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of Elohim (God) more than burnt offerings.* Hosea says Yahuah (the LORD) desired mercy and the knowledge of Elohim (God) more than sacrifice and burnt offerings.$n$),
('mark-12-more-than-all-whole-burnt-offerings-the-scribe-says-what-the-prophets-said',5253,12032,$n$1 Samuel 15:22 — *Behold, to obey is better than sacrifice, and to hearken than the fat of rams.* Samuel says obeying the voice of Yahuah (the LORD) is better than sacrifice and hearkening than the fat of rams.$n$),
('mark-12-more-than-all-whole-burnt-offerings-the-scribe-says-what-the-prophets-said',5254,12032,$n$Micah 6:6 — *shall I come before him with burnt offerings, with calves of a year old.* Micah asks whether he should come before Yahuah (the LORD) with burnt offerings and calves, and is shown what is good.$n$),
('matthew-24-the-beginning-of-sorrows',5263,13006,$n$Matthew 24:5 — *For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and shall deceive many.* Matthew gives the same warning as the answer to the disciples' question about the sign of his coming and of the end of the world.$n$),
('matthew-24-the-beginning-of-sorrows',5264,13006,$n$Luke 21:8 — *And he said, Take heed that ye be not deceived: for many shall come in my name, saying, I am Messiah (Christ); and the time draweth near: go ye not therefore after them.* Luke adds that the deceivers will say the time is near, and Yahusha (Jesus) tells his hearers not to go after them.$n$),
('matthew-24-the-beginning-of-sorrows',5265,13006,$n$2 Corinthians 11:4 — *preacheth another Yahusha (Jesus), whom we have not preached, or if ye receive another spirit.* Paul warns that someone may preach another Yahusha (Jesus), another spirit and another gospel than the one the Corinthians first received.$n$),
('matthew-24-the-beginning-of-sorrows',5266,13006,$n$Galatians 1:6 — *I marvel that ye are so soon removed from him that called you into the grace of Messiah (Christ) unto another gospel:* Paul marvels that the Galatians have so soon turned from the one who called them to another gospel, which is the very deception Mark warns of.$n$),
('mark-13-signs-and-wonders-do-not-vouch-for-the-prophet',5278,13022,$n$Deuteronomy 13:1 — *If there arise among you a prophet, or a dreamer of dreams, and giveth thee a sign or a wonder,* Moses already taught that a prophet's sign or wonder proves nothing by itself, so the warning in Mark sends the hearers back to the test he gave.$n$),
('mark-13-signs-and-wonders-do-not-vouch-for-the-prophet',5279,13022,$n$Revelation 13:13 — *And he doeth great wonders, so that he maketh fire come down from heaven on the earth in the sight of men,* Revelation shows wonders done openly in the sight of men, with fire called down from heaven, as the kind of sign Mark warns about.$n$),
('mark-13-signs-and-wonders-do-not-vouch-for-the-prophet',5280,13022,$n$2 Thessalonians 2:9 — *Even him, whose coming is after the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders,* Paul says the wicked one comes after the working of Satan with power and signs and lying wonders, which is how the elect could be seduced.$n$),
('the-signs-of-the-end-and-the-ten-tribes-gathered-in-2-esdras',5281,13024,$n$Joel 2:10 — *the sun and the moon shall be dark, and the stars shall withdraw their shining.* Joel puts the darkening of the sun, moon and stars at the coming of the day of Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('the-signs-of-the-end-and-the-ten-tribes-gathered-in-2-esdras',5282,13024,$n$Isaiah 13:10 — *the stars of heaven and the constellations thereof shall not give their light.* Isaiah says the stars and constellations, the sun and the moon, will give no light on the day of Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('the-signs-of-the-end-and-the-ten-tribes-gathered-in-2-esdras',5283,13024,$n$Isaiah 34:4 — *the heavens shall be rolled together as a scroll.* Isaiah says the host of heaven will be dissolved and the heavens rolled together like a scroll in the day of Yahuah's (the LORD's) judgment.$n$),
('mark-13-the-son-of-adam-coming-in-the-clouds',5284,13026,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days.* Daniel saw one like the Son of Adam come with the clouds of heaven to the Ancient of days, and Mark names him as Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
('mark-13-the-son-of-adam-coming-in-the-clouds',5285,13026,$n$Matthew 24:30 — *then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of Adam coming in the clouds of heaven.* Matthew adds the sign of the Son of Adam in heaven and the mourning of all the tribes of the earth when they see him come.$n$),
('mark-13-the-son-of-adam-coming-in-the-clouds',5286,13026,$n$Revelation 1:7 — *every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him.* Revelation says he comes with clouds, every eye sees him, those who pierced him included, and the kindreds of the earth wail.$n$),
('mark-13-the-son-of-adam-coming-in-the-clouds',5287,13026,$n$Zechariah 12:10 — *they shall look upon me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son.* Zechariah says they will look upon the one they pierced and mourn for him as one mourns an only son or a firstborn.$n$),
('mark-13-the-gathering-of-the-elect-from-the-four-winds-and-the-ten-tribes-in-2-esdras-baruch-and-sirach',5288,13027,$n$Matthew 13:41 — *The Son of Adam shall send forth his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend, and them which do iniquity;* Matthew has the angels first gather out of the kingdom all things that offend and those who do iniquity.$n$),
('mark-13-the-gathering-of-the-elect-from-the-four-winds-and-the-ten-tribes-in-2-esdras-baruch-and-sirach',5289,13027,$n$Ezekiel 37:21 — *I will take the children of Yashar'el (Israel) from among the heathen, whither they be gone.* Ezekiel says Yahuah (the LORD) will take the children of Yashar'el (Israel) from among the heathen and gather them on every side.$n$),
('mark-13-the-gathering-of-the-elect-from-the-four-winds-and-the-ten-tribes-in-2-esdras-baruch-and-sirach',5290,13027,$n$Deuteronomy 30:4 — *from thence will Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) gather thee, and from thence will he fetch thee.* Moses says that those driven out to the outmost parts of heaven will be gathered and fetched from there by Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('matthew-26-bethany-the-anointing',5302,14003,$n$1 Samuel 16:13 — *Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren.* Samuel anointed David on the head among his brothers, and the Spirit of Yahuah (the LORD) came on him from that day forward.$n$),
('matthew-26-bethany-the-anointing',5303,14003,$n$Matthew 26:6 — *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* Matthew puts the anointing in the same house of Simon the leper in Bethany, where Mark has the woman break the box.$n$),
('john-12-mary-anoints-for-burying-the-poor-ye-have-always-deuteronomy-15',5304,14003,$n$John 12:1 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) six days before the passover came to Bethany, where Lazarus was which had been dead, whom he raised from the dead.* John dates the Bethany supper six days before the passover and, where Lazarus was, tells of Mary anointing the feet of Yahusha (Jesus).$n$),
('last-supper-this-is-my-body-this-is-my-blood-of-the-new-testament',5308,14012,$n$Exodus 12:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt, saying,* Passover begins with Yahuah (the LORD) speaking to Moses and Aaron in Egypt, so the feast Yahusha (Jesus) prepares in Mark is Moses' feast.$n$),
('last-supper-this-is-my-body-this-is-my-blood-of-the-new-testament',5313,14025,$n$Revelation 19:9 — *Blessed are they which are called unto the marriage supper of the Lamb.* Revelation says blessed are those called to the marriage supper of the Lamb, a supper that answers the cup he will drink new.$n$),
('last-supper-this-is-my-body-this-is-my-blood-of-the-new-testament',5314,14025,$n$Isaiah 25:6 — *in this mountain shall Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts) make unto all people a feast of fat things.* Isaiah says Yahuah (the LORD) will make a feast of fat things and refined wines in his mountain, the table the cup looks toward.$n$),
('sanhedrin-trial-tehillim-110-and-daniel-7-self-identification-at-the-kings-own-mouth',5327,14062,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days.* Daniel saw one like the Son of Adam come with the clouds of heaven to the Ancient of days, and Yahusha (Jesus) says he is that one.$n$),
('sanhedrin-trial-tehillim-110-and-daniel-7-self-identification-at-the-kings-own-mouth',5328,14062,$n$Matthew 26:64 — *Hereafter shall ye see the Son of Adam sitting on the right hand of power, and coming in the clouds of heaven.* Matthew has the same words about the Son of Adam at the right hand and in the clouds, after Yahusha (Jesus) says Thou hast said.$n$),
('sanhedrin-trial-tehillim-110-and-daniel-7-self-identification-at-the-kings-own-mouth',5329,14062,$n$Revelation 1:7 — *every eye shall see him, and they also which pierced him: and all kindreds of the earth shall wail because of him.* Revelation says he comes with clouds and every eye sees him, those who pierced him included, while the kindreds of the earth wail.$n$),
('sanhedrin-trial-tehillim-110-and-daniel-7-self-identification-at-the-kings-own-mouth',5330,14061,$n$John 10:36 — *whom the Father hath sanctified, and sent into the world, Thou blasphemest; because I said, I am the Son of Elohim (God).* John shows the same charge of blasphemy for saying I am the Son of Elohim (God), spoken of the one the Father sent into the world.$n$),
('sanhedrin-trial-tehillim-110-and-daniel-7-self-identification-at-the-kings-own-mouth',5331,14061,$n$Hebrews 1:1 — *Elohim (God), who at sundry times and in divers manners spake in time past unto the fathers by the prophets,* Hebrews begins with Elohim (God) speaking by the prophets and then by a Son, who is the express image of the Father's person.$n$),
('mark-15-darkness-at-noon-the-veil-rent-and-the-centurions-confession-amos-8-exodus-26',5355,15039,$n$Matthew 27:54 — *Truly this was the Son of Elohim (God).* Matthew's centurion and his men say the same words after the earthquake, and they feared greatly.$n$),
('mark-15-darkness-at-noon-the-veil-rent-and-the-centurions-confession-amos-8-exodus-26',5356,15039,$n$Luke 23:47 — *Now when the centurion saw what was done, he glorified Elohim (God), saying, Certainly this was a righteous man.* Luke's centurion glorifies Elohim (God) and calls the crucified a righteous man.$n$),
('mark-15-darkness-at-noon-the-veil-rent-and-the-centurions-confession-amos-8-exodus-26',5357,15039,$n$John 19:35 — *and his record is true: and he knoweth that he saith true, that ye might believe.* John's eyewitness record of the death stands beside the centurion's as a second witness at the cross.$n$);
